window.CHEM_MOCK_BANK=[{"topic":1,"name":"Planet Earth","href":"planet-earth.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/23/1A/1","sourceIndex":0,"stem":"Which of the following mixtures can be separated by filtration?","context":"","options":["a mixture of oil and water","a mixture of ethanol and water","a mixture of silver chloride and water","a mixture of sodium chloride and water"],"answer":2,"why":"Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a liquid. Silver chloride is insoluble in water, so it remains as the residue while water passes through the filter paper.","tip":"Ask whether the solid is insoluble before choosing filtration.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/1","sourceIndex":1,"stem":"Which of the following processes is most suitable for extracting sodium chloride from sea water?","context":"","options":["electrolysis","crystallisation","simple distillation","fractional distillation"],"answer":1,"why":"A dissolved solid can be recovered from a solution by crystallisation. Water is removed by evaporation until the solution becomes concentrated; cooling or further evaporation then forms sodium chloride crystals.","tip":"If the required product is the dissolved solute, think crystallisation rather than distillation.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/2","sourceIndex":2,"stem":"Which of the following methods can be used to directly obtain common salt from sea water?","context":"","options":["filtration","dissolution","electrolysis","crystallisation"],"answer":3,"why":"Common salt is dissolved sodium chloride and is obtained directly by crystallisation. Evaporating water concentrates the sea water and allows sodium chloride crystals to form.","tip":"“Directly obtain” rules out processes that chemically change the salt.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/1","sourceIndex":4,"stem":"A flame test conducted for a sample gives a brick-red flame. The sample may contain","context":"","options":["chalk.","quartz.","graphite.","rock salt."],"answer":0,"why":"Calcium ions give a brick-red flame in a flame test. Chalk consists mainly of calcium carbonate, CaCO₃, and therefore contains calcium ions.","tip":"Memorise: calcium - brick red; sodium - golden yellow; potassium - lilac.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/19","sourceIndex":5,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning anhydrous copper(II) sulphate powder are correct?","context":"(1) It is white in colour.\n(2) It dissolves in water to give a blue solution.\n(3) It can be obtained from heating hydrated copper(II) sulphate crystals.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"Anhydrous copper(II) sulphate is white and becomes hydrated in the presence of water. All three statements are correct: the anhydrous solid is white, aqueous copper(II) ions form a blue solution, and heating hydrated crystals removes water of crystallisation.","tip":"Do not confuse white anhydrous CuSO₄ with blue hydrated CuSO₄·5H₂O.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/1","sourceIndex":6,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning CO₂(g) is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["It can turn lime water milky.","It can be used to make dry ice.","It can be produced by adding marble to water.","It generally has a higher percentage in air in urban areas than that in rural areas."],"answer":2,"why":"Marble is calcium carbonate and does not release carbon dioxide when added to water. Carbon dioxide is produced when calcium carbonate reacts with an acid, not with water alone.","tip":"Carbonates need acid to release carbon dioxide.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/3","sourceIndex":7,"stem":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["Calcium carbonate solid can be obtained from passing carbon dioxide gas into lime water.","Calcium hydroxide solid can be obtained directly from strong heating of marble.","Hydrogen gas can be obtained directly from electrolysis of sea water.","Nitrogen gas can be obtained from fractional distillation of liquefied air."],"answer":1,"why":"Strong heating of marble produces calcium oxide, not calcium hydroxide. Marble is calcium carbonate. Thermal decomposition gives quicklime, CaO. Calcium hydroxide is formed only after CaO reacts with water.","tip":"Strong heating: limestone → quicklime. Adding water: quicklime → slaked lime.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/19","sourceIndex":8,"stem":"In an experiment, marble is heated in a boiling tube and the gas evolved is passed into a test tube with lime water. Which of the following statements concerning the experiment is / are correct?","context":"(1) The marble turns brick red upon heating.\n(2) The lime water turns milky initially but eventually becomes a colourless solution.\n(3) If marble is replaced by chalk, a similar observation would be obtained.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Both marble and chalk contain calcium carbonate and give carbon dioxide on strong heating. Statement (1) is false: marble does not burn with a brick-red flame merely because it is heated. Statement (2) is true because excess CO₂ converts insoluble CaCO₃ into soluble calcium hydrogencarbonate. Statement (3) is true because chalk is also mainly CaCO₃.","tip":"Distinguish heating a calcium compound from conducting a flame test.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/1","sourceIndex":9,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning quicklime is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["Heating marble strongly can form quicklime.","Reacting quicklime with sulphur dioxide can form calcium sulphate.","Reacting quicklime with carbon dioxide can form calcium carbonate.","A large amount of heat evolves when quicklime is put into water."],"answer":1,"why":"Quicklime is calcium oxide, CaO. Calcium oxide reacts with sulphur dioxide to form calcium sulphite, CaSO₃, not calcium sulphate, CaSO₄.","tip":"SO₂ commonly gives sulphite; oxidation is needed to form sulphate.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/2","sourceIndex":10,"stem":"A white solid X gives a brick-red flame in a flame test. When X(s) is added to dilute hydrochloric acid, a colourless gas is given out. Which of the following compounds may X be?","context":"","options":["calcium sulphate","potassium sulphate","calcium carbonate","potassium carbonate"],"answer":2,"why":"Brick red identifies calcium ions; effervescence with acid identifies carbonate ions. Calcium carbonate contains both Ca²⁺ and CO₃²⁻. The gas is carbon dioxide.","tip":"Combine both test results; do not use only one observation.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/20","sourceIndex":11,"stem":"Which of the following processes can be used to distinguish between K₂CO₃(aq) and CaCl₂(aq)?","context":"(1) performing a flame test\n(2) adding dilute hydrochloric acid\n(3) adding acidified silver nitrate solution","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"A valid distinguishing test must give different observations for the two solutions. All three work: K⁺ gives a lilac flame while Ca²⁺ gives brick red; carbonate effervesces with acid while calcium chloride does not; after acidification, chloride gives a white AgCl precipitate while carbonate is removed as CO₂.","tip":"For acidified silver nitrate, acid removes carbonate interference before testing chloride.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/23","sourceIndex":12,"stem":"Which of the following can distinguish a sample of limestone powder from a sample of table salt?","context":"(1) adding water\n(2) performing a flame test\n(3) adding dilute hydrochloric acid","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"Limestone is mainly CaCO₃; table salt is mainly NaCl. All three tests distinguish them: limestone is insoluble while table salt dissolves; calcium gives brick red while sodium gives golden yellow; limestone effervesces with acid while table salt shows no observable change.","tip":"State both observations whenever a question asks how to distinguish two substances.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/13","sourceIndex":13,"stem":"Which of the following statements about limestone powder and quicklime powder is / are correct?","context":"(1) Both of them react with dilute hydrochloric acid to give salt and water only.\n(2) Both of them give a brick-red flame in flame test.\n(3) Both of them give a colourless gas when heated strongly.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"Limestone is CaCO₃ and quicklime is CaO. Only statement (2) is correct. Both contain calcium and give a brick-red flame. Limestone also gives CO₂ when reacting with hydrochloric acid, so statement (1) is false. Limestone releases CO₂ on strong heating, but quicklime does not, so statement (3) is false.","tip":"Compare CaCO₃ and CaO separately for each statement.","strand":""}]},{"topic":2,"name":"Microscopic World I","href":"microscopic-world-i.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/15/1A/15","sourceIndex":0,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning “atom” is correct?","context":"","options":["All atoms do not carry net charges.","Mass is evenly distributed within an atom.","All atoms consist of protons, neutrons and electrons.","For all elements, atoms of the same element have the same mass number."],"answer":0,"why":"Atoms are electrically neutral overall. An atom has equal numbers of protons and electrons, so it carries no net charge.","tip":"An atom is neutral; an ion carries a net charge.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/1","sourceIndex":1,"stem":"Which of the following atoms has the smallest number of neutrons?","context":"","options":["⁶³Cu","⁵⁹Co","⁵⁸Ni","⁵⁷Fe"],"answer":2,"why":"Number of neutrons = mass number − atomic number. Cu: 63−29=34; Co: 59−27=32; Ni: 58−28=30; Fe: 57−26=31. Nickel-58 has the fewest neutrons.","tip":"Use the element’s atomic number, not the mass number, for protons.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/15","sourceIndex":2,"stem":"Which statements concerning a ¹³¹I atom and a ¹³¹Xe atom are correct?","context":"(1) They have the same number of protons.\n(2) They have different numbers of neutrons.\n(3) They have different numbers of outermost-shell electrons.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Different elements have different proton and electron arrangements. I and Xe have the same mass number but different atomic numbers, so they have different neutron numbers. Their outermost-shell electron numbers also differ.","tip":"Same mass number does not mean same element.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/16","sourceIndex":3,"stem":"Consider ⁷⁴₃₂Ge, ⁷⁵₃₃As and ⁷⁶₃₄Se. Which statements are correct?","context":"(1) They have different numbers of protons.\n(2) They have different numbers of neutrons.\n(3) They have different numbers of outermost-shell electrons.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":2,"why":"Atomic number gives protons; group gives outermost-shell electrons. Their proton numbers are 32, 33 and 34. Each has 42 neutrons. Ge, As and Se have 4, 5 and 6 outermost-shell electrons.","tip":"For a set of nuclides, calculate each neutron number explicitly.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/12","sourceIndex":5,"stem":"Both radium and calcium are in the same group. Which statement is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["Radium is a good conductor of electricity in the solid state.","Radium atoms readily donate electrons to form Ra²⁺ ions.","Both radium and calcium become tarnished after exposure to air.","Radium is less reactive than calcium."],"answer":3,"why":"Group II metals become more reactive down the group. Radium lies below calcium, so radium is more reactive, not less reactive.","tip":"Down Group II, outer electrons are lost more readily.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/1","sourceIndex":6,"stem":"Which pair of atomic numbers corresponds to elements with similar chemical properties?","context":"","options":["4 and 14","8 and 18","9 and 35","19 and 38"],"answer":2,"why":"Elements in the same group have similar chemical properties. Atomic numbers 9 and 35 have electronic arrangements 2,7 and 2,8,18,7. Both are Group VII elements.","tip":"Write electronic arrangements and compare outermost-shell electrons.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/2","sourceIndex":8,"stem":"How many neutrons and electrons are in a ⁵¹₂₃X³⁺ ion?","context":"","options":["23 neutrons, 20 electrons","28 neutrons, 23 electrons","28 neutrons, 20 electrons","51 neutrons, 23 electrons"],"answer":2,"why":"Mass number = protons + neutrons; a 3+ ion has lost 3 electrons. Neutrons = 51−23=28. A neutral X atom has 23 electrons, so X³⁺ has 20.","tip":"Ion formation changes electrons only, never protons or neutrons.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/4","sourceIndex":10,"stem":"Scandium nitrate is Sc(NO₃)₃. What is the most likely formula of scandium phosphate?","context":"","options":["Sc₂(PO₄)₃","ScPO₄","Sc(PO₄)₂","Sc(PO₄)₃"],"answer":1,"why":"Ionic compounds are electrically neutral. Three nitrate ions show that scandium is Sc³⁺. Phosphate is PO₄³⁻, so they combine in a 1:1 ratio.","tip":"Infer an unknown ion charge from a known formula, then balance charges.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/3","sourceIndex":11,"stem":"Q is in Group II and forms Q₃R₂ with element R. Which group does R belong to?","context":"","options":["Group III","Group V","Group VI","Group VII"],"answer":1,"why":"The total positive and negative charges in an ionic compound must balance. Three Q²⁺ ions contribute +6, so two R ions contribute −6 and each is R³⁻. An element forming a 3− ion belongs to Group V.","tip":"Use the subscripts to balance total charge.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/1","sourceIndex":12,"stem":"X and Y form X₂Y. If the ions of X and Y have the same electronic arrangement, which compound may it be?","context":"","options":["lithium oxide","aluminium oxide","potassium sulphide","magnesium chloride"],"answer":2,"why":"The formula must be X₂Y and the two ions must be isoelectronic. K₂S has the required formula. K⁺ and S²⁻ both have electronic arrangement 2,8,8.","tip":"Check both conditions: formula ratio and electron arrangement.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/3","sourceIndex":13,"stem":"Which of the following substances is an electrolyte?","context":"","options":["sodium chloride","silicon dioxide","methanol","mercury"],"answer":0,"why":"An electrolyte conducts electricity when molten or in aqueous solution by mobile ions. Sodium chloride is ionic; its ions are mobile when molten or dissolved in water.","tip":"A metal conductor is not automatically an electrolyte.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/16","sourceIndex":16,"stem":"Which statements concerning helium are correct?","context":"(1) Helium is chemically inert.\n(2) Helium exists as diatomic molecules.\n(3) The outermost electron shell of a helium atom has an octet structure.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Helium is inert because its first shell has a stable duplet. Helium is monatomic, not diatomic, and has 2 outermost-shell electrons rather than an octet.","tip":"Helium is the exception to the octet description of Group 0.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/5","sourceIndex":17,"stem":"X is one of the first twenty elements and forms a stable XH₄⁺(aq) ion. Which group does X most likely belong to?","context":"","options":["Group III","Group IV","Group V","Group VI"],"answer":2,"why":"The ammonium-type XH₄⁺ ion is formed by a Group V hydride accepting H⁺. Among the first twenty elements, nitrogen forms the stable ammonium ion NH₄⁺. Nitrogen is in Group V.","tip":"Recognise NH₄⁺ as the familiar XH₄⁺ ion.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/2","sourceIndex":18,"stem":"Neon is a gas at room temperature and pressure because","context":"","options":["neon is chemically inert.","neon molecules are monatomic.","the attractive force between neon atoms is weak.","the outermost electron shell of a neon atom has an octet structure."],"answer":2,"why":"Physical state depends on interparticle attraction. Only weak van der Waals forces act between neon atoms, so little energy is needed to separate them.","tip":"For melting and boiling, focus on attractions between particles.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/15","sourceIndex":19,"stem":"Argon is a gas at room temperature and pressure because","context":"","options":["its outermost electron shell has an octet structure.","the attractive force between argon atoms is weak.","argon molecules are monatomic.","argon is chemically inert."],"answer":1,"why":"Weak interatomic attraction gives argon a low boiling point. Only weak van der Waals forces act between argon atoms, so argon remains gaseous at room temperature.","tip":"Chemical stability and physical state require different explanations.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/2","sourceIndex":20,"stem":"Which compound has a giant ionic structure?","context":"","options":["N₂O₄","HNO₃","NCl₃","NH₄NO₃"],"answer":3,"why":"A lattice of cations and anions is a giant ionic structure. Ammonium nitrate consists of NH₄⁺ and NO₃⁻ ions arranged in an ionic lattice.","tip":"Polyatomic ions can still form an ionic compound.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/1","sourceIndex":21,"stem":"Which substance CANNOT conduct electricity?","context":"","options":["Pt(s)","PbBr₂(l)","C(graphite)","CH₃CH₂OH(l)"],"answer":3,"why":"Electrical conduction requires mobile charged particles. Liquid ethanol has neither mobile ions nor delocalised electrons.","tip":"For ionic compounds, always check the physical state.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/8","sourceIndex":23,"stem":"Silicon carbide has a crystal structure similar to diamond. Why is it very hard?","context":"","options":["It has a high melting point.","Silicon and carbon atoms form triple bonds.","It has a giant network structure with strong covalent bonds.","Both silicon and carbon atoms have four outermost-shell electrons."],"answer":2,"why":"Hardness of a giant covalent solid comes from strong bonds throughout the network. Many strong covalent bonds extend in all directions and must be broken to deform the solid.","tip":"Link property → structure → bonding.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/2","sourceIndex":24,"stem":"Which statement concerning quartz is correct?","context":"","options":["Quartz is soluble in hexane.","Quartz consists of SiO₂ molecules.","Quartz conducts electricity by delocalised electrons.","Quartz is hard because it has a giant covalent network structure."],"answer":3,"why":"Quartz is a giant covalent solid. Strong Si–O covalent bonds extend throughout the network, making quartz hard.","tip":"A formula such as SiO₂ can give a ratio, not a molecule.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/1","sourceIndex":25,"stem":"Which statement about silicon and silicon dioxide is correct?","context":"","options":["Silicon is a good conductor of heat.","Silicon exists as simple molecules.","SiO₂ is hard at room temperature.","SiO₂ dissolves in water to give an acidic solution."],"answer":2,"why":"Silicon dioxide has a giant covalent structure. Strong covalent bonds throughout its network make SiO₂ hard.","tip":"Do not infer molecular structure from a short empirical formula.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/5","sourceIndex":26,"stem":"Why is quartz (SiO₂) harder than dry ice (CO₂)?","context":"","options":["Silicon atoms are larger than carbon atoms.","Silicon atoms have more electrons than carbon atoms.","Quartz has a giant network, but dry ice consists of discrete molecules.","The Si–O bond is strong, but the C–O bond is weak."],"answer":2,"why":"Quartz is giant covalent; dry ice is simple molecular. Deforming quartz requires breaking strong covalent bonds in a network. Melting or deforming dry ice mainly overcomes weak intermolecular forces.","tip":"Compare bonds broken in the process, not merely bond strengths inside molecules.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/24","sourceIndex":27,"stem":"Mercury has good electrical conductivity at room temperature. Mercury has delocalised electrons. Choose the best answer.","context":"","options":["Both statements are true and the second correctly explains the first.","Both statements are true but the second does not explain the first.","The first is false but the second is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":0,"why":"Metals conduct because delocalised electrons move through the structure. Mercury is a liquid metal at room temperature and its mobile delocalised electrons carry charge.","tip":"In assertion–reason questions, test truth first, then causality.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/1","sourceIndex":28,"stem":"Which substance has delocalised electrons?","context":"","options":["NaCl(s)","Br₂(l)","Hg(l)","I₂(s)"],"answer":2,"why":"Metallic structures contain delocalised electrons. Mercury is a metal even though it is liquid at room temperature.","tip":"Physical state does not change whether an element is metallic.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/21","sourceIndex":29,"stem":"Which solids have delocalised electrons in their structures?","context":"(1) graphite\n(2) silicon\n(3) silver","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":2,"why":"Graphite and metals contain mobile delocalised electrons. Each carbon in graphite contributes one delocalised electron; silver has metallic delocalised electrons. Silicon’s electrons are localised in covalent bonds.","tip":"Not every giant covalent solid conducts; graphite is the key exception.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/1","sourceIndex":30,"stem":"A species melts at 146 °C, dissolves in water, and its solution does not conduct electricity. Which structure would it have?","context":"","options":["giant ionic","giant metallic","giant covalent","simple molecular"],"answer":3,"why":"Low melting point and a non-conducting solution indicate a molecular nonelectrolyte. A 146 °C melting point is relatively low. Dissolving without producing ions gives a non-conducting solution, consistent with simple molecules.","tip":"Use all three properties together.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/14","sourceIndex":34,"stem":"Which statements about Group 0 elements are correct?","context":"(1) Their boiling points increase down the group.\n(2) None of them are present in air.\n(3) All have eight electrons in the outermost shell.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Van der Waals forces strengthen down Group 0. Boiling points increase down the group as atomic size and electron number increase. Noble gases are present in air, and helium has a stable duplet, not an octet.","tip":"Remember helium as the Group 0 duplet exception.","strand":""}]},{"topic":3,"name":"Metals","href":"metals.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/13/1A/5","sourceIndex":1,"stem":"Which method can obtain magnesium from magnesium compounds?","context":"","options":["Electrolysis of a molten magnesium compound","Electrolysis of an aqueous magnesium compound","Heating magnesium oxide with carbon","Strongly heating magnesium oxide"],"answer":0,"why":"Very reactive metals are extracted by electrolysis of molten compounds. Magnesium is above carbon in the reactivity series. In aqueous electrolysis, water is reduced instead of Mg²⁺; a molten compound is required.","tip":"Metals above carbon require molten electrolysis.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/17","sourceIndex":2,"stem":"Which metal oxides can be reduced to metals by heating with carbon using a Bunsen burner?","context":"(1) lead(II) oxide\n(2) magnesium oxide\n(3) copper(II) oxide","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":2,"why":"Carbon reduces oxides of metals below carbon in the reactivity series. Lead and copper are less reactive than carbon, but magnesium is more reactive and its oxide is too stable.","tip":"Compare each metal with carbon in the reactivity series.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/9","sourceIndex":4,"stem":"Which process would NOT produce a metal?","context":"","options":["Heating zinc oxide","Heating copper(II) oxide with carbon","Electrolysis of molten lithium chloride","Heating iron(III) oxide with carbon monoxide"],"answer":0,"why":"Zinc oxide does not decompose to zinc by heating alone. ZnO needs a reducing agent such as carbon, carbon monoxide or hydrogen. The other processes produce Cu, Li and Fe respectively.","tip":"Heating alone only works for oxides of very unreactive metals.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/18","sourceIndex":6,"stem":"Potassium is added to water containing phenolphthalein. Which statements are correct?","context":"(1) An exothermic reaction occurs.\n(2) A colourless solution is formed.\n(3) The metal burns with a lilac flame.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":1,"why":"Potassium reacts vigorously and exothermically with water. The heat may ignite potassium/hydrogen with a lilac flame. KOH forms, so phenolphthalein turns pink; statement (2) is false.","tip":"Phenolphthalein is pink in alkaline solution.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/20","sourceIndex":7,"stem":"Sodium is added to water containing universal indicator. Which statements are correct?","context":"(1) Sodium moves quickly on the water surface.\n(2) The resulting solution is red.\n(3) The reaction is exothermic.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":2,"why":"Sodium forms an alkaline solution in an exothermic reaction. Sodium moves on the surface and releases heat. NaOH makes universal indicator blue/purple, not red.","tip":"Group I metal + water gives alkaline hydroxide + hydrogen.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/21","sourceIndex":8,"stem":"What is observed when calcium granules are placed in cold water?","context":"(1) A cloudy mixture forms.\n(2) The test tube becomes warm.\n(3) Colourless gas bubbles form.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"Calcium reacts exothermically with water to form Ca(OH)₂ and H₂. Slightly soluble Ca(OH)₂ makes the mixture cloudy; heat is released and colourless hydrogen bubbles form.","tip":"Slight solubility of calcium hydroxide explains the cloudiness.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/3","sourceIndex":11,"stem":"Y reacts vigorously with water, forms Y₂O, and has five occupied electron shells. What may Y be?","context":"","options":["silver","caesium","strontium","rubidium"],"answer":3,"why":"Y is a Period 5 Group I metal. Y₂O indicates Y⁺, vigorous reaction with water indicates Group I, and five occupied shells indicate Period 5: rubidium.","tip":"Use formula, reactivity and period information together.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/12","sourceIndex":12,"stem":"For xNH₃(g) + yO₂(g) → xNO(g) + zH₂O(g), which coefficients are correct?","context":"","options":["x=2, y=3, z=3","x=2, y=3, z=6","x=4, y=5, z=4","x=4, y=5, z=6"],"answer":3,"why":"Balance each element without changing formulae. Balancing N, H then O gives 4NH₃ + 5O₂ → 4NO + 6H₂O.","tip":"Balance oxygen last when it appears in several products.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/9","sourceIndex":13,"stem":"For xH₂S + 2KMnO₄ + 3H₂SO₄ → xS + yH₂O + zMnSO₄ + K₂SO₄, which x and y are correct?","context":"","options":["x=3, y=6","x=5, y=8","x=5, y=10","x=7, y=10"],"answer":1,"why":"Balance atoms systematically. Mn gives z=2. Oxygen then gives y=8, and hydrogen gives x=5.","tip":"Use an element appearing in only one species on each side first.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/21","sourceIndex":14,"stem":"Which statements show that Y is more reactive than gold?","context":"(1) Y reacts with dilute HCl while gold does not.\n(2) Y displaces copper from Cu(NO₃)₂ while gold does not.\n(3) Gold can be extracted physically from its ore while Y cannot.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"Reaction, displacement and extraction evidence can compare reactivity. Each observation places Y above gold: Y is above hydrogen, Y is above copper while gold is below it, and gold occurs native more readily than Y.","tip":"Translate each observation into a reactivity-series position.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/14","sourceIndex":15,"stem":"Which metal W, X, Y or Z has the lowest reactivity?","context":"(1) Heating W oxide gives W.\n(2) Heating X in steam gives a colourless gas.\n(3) Putting Y in CH₃CO₂H gives a colourless gas.\n(4) Putting Z in CuSO₄ gives a reddish-brown solid.","options":["W","X","Y","Z"],"answer":0,"why":"An oxide decomposed by heat alone belongs to a very unreactive metal. W may be silver or mercury. X reacts with steam, Y with acid, and Z displaces copper, so all X, Y and Z are more reactive.","tip":"The easier an oxide is reduced, the less reactive the metal.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/9","sourceIndex":16,"stem":"Only Y reacts in NaCl(aq), and only oxide X releases a gas on strong heating. What is the decreasing reactivity order?","context":"","options":["Y > Z > X","X > Y > Z","Y > X > Z","Z > Y > X"],"answer":0,"why":"Reaction with water identifies the most reactive; easy oxide decomposition identifies the least reactive. Y reacts with water and is most reactive. X oxide decomposes most readily, so X is least reactive. Therefore Y > Z > X.","tip":"Convert each test into a “most” or “least” statement first.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/4","sourceIndex":17,"stem":"Only oxide M gives a metallic lustre on heating; only carbonate R shows no change. What is the increasing reactivity order?","context":"","options":["R < Q < M","R < M < Q","M < R < Q","M < Q < R"],"answer":3,"why":"More reactive metals form more thermally stable compounds. M oxide decomposes to metal, so M is least reactive. R carbonate is most stable, so R is most reactive. Hence M < Q < R.","tip":"Compound stability generally increases with metal reactivity.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/4","sourceIndex":19,"stem":"If 8.0 g SO₂ contains n molecules, how many molecules are in 2.0 g O₂?","context":"","options":["2.0n","4.0n","0.25n","0.50n"],"answer":3,"why":"Number of molecules is proportional to moles. 8.0/64.0 = 0.125 mol SO₂, while 2.0/32.0 = 0.0625 mol O₂, half as many moles and molecules.","tip":"Convert each sample to moles before comparing particle numbers.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/9","sourceIndex":21,"stem":"Which statement about an aluminium ore consisting mainly of Al₂O₃ is correct?","context":"","options":["Carbon can extract aluminium from it.","Its abundance in the crust is very low.","It contains more than 55% aluminium by mass.","Aluminium extraction became possible through applying electricity."],"answer":3,"why":"Aluminium is extracted by electrolysis, enabled by electrical technology. Aluminium is too reactive for carbon reduction. Aluminium ores are abundant, and Al₂O₃ contains about 52.9% Al, not more than 55%.","tip":"High-reactivity metals require electrolysis.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/3","sourceIndex":22,"stem":"What is the mass of oxygen in 24.0 g of CuSO₄·5H₂O?","context":"","options":["6.2 g","9.6 g","13.8 g","21.7 g"],"answer":2,"why":"Count oxygen in both the sulphate ion and water of crystallisation. The hydrate has 9 oxygen atoms per formula unit. Its molar mass is 249.6 g mol⁻¹, and oxygen contributes 144.0 g.","tip":"A dot formula includes atoms on both sides of the dot.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/5","sourceIndex":23,"stem":"Hydrated salt X·nH₂O contains 51.16% water. M(X)=120.3 g mol⁻¹. What is n?","context":"","options":["2","5","7","10"],"answer":2,"why":"Set the water mass fraction equal to the given percentage. 18n/(120.3+18n)=0.5116 gives n≈7.","tip":"Include the water mass in the total molar mass denominator.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/8","sourceIndex":24,"stem":"39.2 g of a rubidium oxide contains 28.5 g Rb. What is its empirical formula?","context":"","options":["RbO","RbO₂","Rb₂O","Rb₂O₂"],"answer":1,"why":"Empirical formula comes from the simplest mole ratio. O mass is 10.7 g. Moles: Rb=28.5/85.5=0.333; O=10.7/16=0.669, giving Rb:O≈1:2.","tip":"Subtract to find the oxygen mass first.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/13","sourceIndex":25,"stem":"2.66 g titanium forms 4.44 g oxide. What is the empirical formula?","context":"","options":["TiO","Ti₂O₃","Ti₃O₄","TiO₂"],"answer":3,"why":"Use moles of metal and gained oxygen. O mass=1.78 g. Ti=2.66/47.9=0.0555 mol; O=1.78/16=0.111 mol, a 1:2 ratio.","tip":"Mass gain during oxidation is the oxygen mass.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/6","sourceIndex":26,"stem":"2.21 g oxide of X is heated; 1.97 g metal remains. Ar(X)=197. What is the oxide formula?","context":"","options":["XO","XO₂","X₂O","X₂O₃"],"answer":3,"why":"Find moles of remaining metal and oxygen lost. X=1.97/197=0.0100 mol. O=(2.21−1.97)/16=0.0150 mol. X:O=2:3.","tip":"The lost mass is oxygen because the oxide decomposes to metal.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/3","sourceIndex":27,"stem":"An oxide contains 55.0% metal M by mass. Ar(M)=39.1. What is its formula?","context":"","options":["MO₂","M₂O","M₂O₂","M₂O₃"],"answer":0,"why":"Assume 100 g and convert each element mass to moles. M: 55/39.1=1.406 mol; O:45/16=2.813 mol. The ratio is 1:2.","tip":"A 100 g basis turns percentages directly into grams.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/3","sourceIndex":28,"stem":"A hydrocarbon gives 17.6 g CO₂ and 3.6 g H₂O on complete combustion. What is its empirical formula?","context":"","options":["CH","CH₂","C₂H₂","C₂H₅"],"answer":0,"why":"Combustion products reveal moles of C and H atoms. 17.6/44=0.400 mol C. 3.6/18=0.200 mol H₂O=0.400 mol H atoms. C:H=1:1.","tip":"Empirical formula must be in the simplest whole-number ratio.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/13","sourceIndex":29,"stem":"30.00 g W burns to give 33.75 g H₂O and 41.25 g CO₂. Which compound may W be?","context":"","options":["HCOOH","CH₂=CHCOOH","CH₃OH","CH₂(OH)CH₂OH"],"answer":2,"why":"Combustion analysis gives the empirical composition. C mass=11.25 g, H mass=3.75 g and O mass=15.00 g. Mole ratio C:H:O=1:4:1, matching CH₃OH.","tip":"Find oxygen in W by mass difference.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/19","sourceIndex":30,"stem":"X is 26.8% by mass in K₂XO₄. Which statements about X are correct?","context":"(1) X is a transition metal.\n(2) X is in Group VI.\n(3) X is in Period 4.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":2,"why":"The percentage composition identifies X as chromium. Solving x/(78.2+x+64)=0.268 gives Ar(X)≈52.0, chromium. Cr is a Period 4 transition metal; the syllabus group statement (2) is not accepted here.","tip":"Determine the element before applying periodic-table classifications.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/4","sourceIndex":31,"stem":"N mol Fe₂O₃ reacts with 2N mol CO: Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂. How many moles Fe form?","context":"","options":["N","2N","2N/3","4N/3"],"answer":3,"why":"Identify the limiting reagent using the mole ratio. 2N mol CO can react with 2N/3 mol Fe₂O₃. This forms twice as many moles of Fe: 4N/3.","tip":"The smaller stoichiometric amount determines product yield.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/3","sourceIndex":32,"stem":"What theoretical mass of H₂ forms when 13.50 g Al reacts with excess dilute HCl?","context":"","options":["0.67 g","0.75 g","1.00 g","1.50 g"],"answer":3,"why":"Use the balanced equation and mole ratio. 13.50/27.0=0.500 mol Al. From 2Al→3H₂, this gives 0.750 mol H₂=1.50 g.","tip":"H₂ has molar mass 2.0 g mol⁻¹.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/6","sourceIndex":33,"stem":"2M(NO₃)₂ → 2MO + 4NO₂ + O₂. A 0.164 g sample gives 2.50×10⁻³ mol gases. What is M?","context":"","options":["Mg","Ca","Sr","Ba"],"answer":1,"why":"Two moles nitrate produce five moles gas. Moles M(NO₃)₂=2/5×2.50×10⁻³=1.00×10⁻³ mol. Its molar mass is 164, so Ar(M)=164−124=40: calcium.","tip":"Use the total gas coefficient, not only NO₂.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/6","sourceIndex":34,"stem":"2.53 g NaHCO₃ is heated and 1.59 g solid remains. Which equation matches?","context":"","options":["NaHCO₃ → NaOH + CO₂","2NaHCO₃ → Na₂O + 2CO₂ + H₂","2NaHCO₃ → Na₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂","2NaHCO₃ → Na₂O + H₂O + 2CO₂"],"answer":2,"why":"Compare predicted solid mass with the measured residue. 2.53 g NaHCO₃ is 0.0301 mol. It forms 0.0151 mol Na₂CO₃, mass≈1.60 g, matching 1.59 g.","tip":"The solid residue is sodium carbonate.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/5","sourceIndex":35,"stem":"Silver is extracted from Ag₂S at 85% yield. What Ag₂S mass is needed for 15.0 g Ag?","context":"","options":["14.6 g","17.2 g","20.3 g","34.5 g"],"answer":2,"why":"Correct the required product amount for percentage yield. 15.0 g Ag is 0.139 mol actual. Theoretical Ag needed=0.139/0.85 mol; Ag₂S moles are half this, giving 20.3 g.","tip":"Divide actual yield by fractional yield to get theoretical amount.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/23","sourceIndex":36,"stem":"Iron and copper are separately immersed in hexane: iron corrodes faster. Iron oxidises more readily than copper. Choose the best answer.","context":"","options":["Both true; second explains first","Both true; second does not explain first","First false; second true","Both false"],"answer":2,"why":"Corrosion needs suitable environmental conditions as well as reactivity. Neither metal corrodes in hexane because water/oxygen conditions are absent. However, iron is more readily oxidised than copper.","tip":"Do not infer actual corrosion from reactivity alone.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/5","sourceIndex":37,"stem":"An iron nail corrodes in yellow gel containing NaCl, K₃Fe(CN)₆ and phenolphthalein. Which colour is NOT observed?","context":"","options":["blue","pink","grey","yellow"],"answer":2,"why":"Corrosion indicators show Fe²⁺ and OH⁻ regions. Fe²⁺ with ferricyanide gives blue; OH⁻ turns phenolphthalein pink; unchanged gel remains yellow. Grey is not produced.","tip":"Link each indicator to its ion before choosing a colour.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/7","sourceIndex":38,"stem":"Which statement concerning iron and magnesium is correct?","context":"","options":["Iron is ductile but magnesium is not.","Iron corrodes less readily than magnesium.","Magnesium is more abundant than iron in the crust.","Both have more than one oxidation number in their oxides."],"answer":1,"why":"Magnesium is more reactive and oxidises more readily than iron. Therefore magnesium corrodes more readily. Both are ductile metals; iron is more abundant in the stated comparison; Mg normally has only +2.","tip":"Reactivity generally predicts ease of oxidation.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/5","sourceIndex":43,"stem":"Why does tin plating prevent iron cans from rusting?","context":"","options":["Tin gives sacrificial protection.","The tin layer excludes air.","Tin is above iron in the reactivity series.","Tin and iron form a non-corroding alloy."],"answer":1,"why":"An intact coating prevents iron contacting oxygen and water. Tin is less reactive than iron, so it is not sacrificial. Its protection is a physical barrier.","tip":"Distinguish barrier protection from sacrificial protection.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/15","sourceIndex":46,"stem":"Which method CANNOT prevent corrosion of an iron object?","context":"","options":["Immerse it in vinegar","Immerse it in oil","Connect it to zinc","Connect it to the negative pole of a cell"],"answer":0,"why":"Acidic electrolyte accelerates iron corrosion. Vinegar is acidic and promotes iron dissolution. Oil is a barrier; zinc is sacrificial; the negative pole supplies electrons for cathodic protection.","tip":"A conducting acidic solution is not a protective coating.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/15","sourceIndex":47,"stem":"Which methods slow corrosion of an iron object?","context":"(1) Connect it to lead.\n(2) Completely copper-plate it.\n(3) Connect it to the cathode of a chemical cell.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"A complete coating blocks air and water. Complete copper plating protects as a barrier. Lead is less reactive and makes iron the anode if connected; the stated chemical-cell cathode connection does not provide the required protective electron supply in this setup.","tip":"For plating with a less reactive metal, the coating must remain complete.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/18","sourceIndex":49,"stem":"Why does aluminium oxide protect aluminium while iron oxide does not protect iron?","context":"(1) Aluminium oxide adheres firmly; iron oxide adheres loosely.\n(2) Aluminium oxide is insoluble; iron oxide is soluble.\n(3) Aluminium oxide is giant ionic; iron oxide is not.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"A compact, adherent oxide layer blocks further attack. Statement (1) is correct. Both oxides are insoluble and both have giant ionic structures, so (2) and (3) are false.","tip":"Protective oxide layers must be continuous and firmly attached.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/23","sourceIndex":50,"stem":"During anodisation, aluminium oxide is reduced to metal. Anodisation enhances corrosion resistance. Choose the best answer.","context":"","options":["Both true; second explains first","Both true; second does not explain first","First false; second true","Both false"],"answer":2,"why":"Anodisation oxidises aluminium and thickens its oxide layer. The first statement is false: oxide is formed/thickened, not reduced. The thicker adherent layer improves corrosion resistance, so the second is true.","tip":"The anode is the site of oxidation.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/2","sourceIndex":51,"stem":"How many oxygen atoms are present in 9.98 g quartz, SiO₂?","context":"","options":["0.83 × 10²³","1.00 × 10²³","1.33 × 10²³","2.00 × 10²³"],"answer":3,"why":"Each SiO₂ formula unit contains two oxygen atoms. M(SiO₂)=60.1. Moles SiO₂=9.98/60.1=0.166; moles O atoms=0.332, giving 2.00×10²³ atoms.","tip":"Convert formula units to atoms using the subscript.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/3","sourceIndex":52,"stem":"M forms MO, reacts with dilute HCl to give H₂, and shows no immediate change with cold water. What may M be?","context":"","options":["Al","Ca","Cu","Zn"],"answer":3,"why":"Match oxide formula and reactivity observations. Zinc forms ZnO, reacts with dilute HCl, and has no immediate observable reaction with cold water.","tip":"All three observations must fit the same metal.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/4","sourceIndex":53,"stem":"35.6 g hydrated Na₂CO₃ contains 25.8% sodium. What mass is lost on complete heating?","context":"","options":["2.4 g","14.4 g","21.2 g","24.4 g"],"answer":1,"why":"Use sodium percentage to determine the water fraction. 46/(106+18n)=0.258 gives n≈4. The anhydrous mass is 35.6×106/178=21.2 g, so water lost=14.4 g.","tip":"Sodium remains in the residue; water is the lost mass.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/7","sourceIndex":54,"stem":"Y and Z are same-group metals (atomic numbers ≤20): YSO₄ + Z(NO₃)₂ → ZSO₄(s) + Y(NO₃)₂. Which statement is correct?","context":"","options":["Both Y and Z do not react with cold water.","Atomic number of Y is smaller than Z.","SO₄²⁻ and NO₃⁻ are both spectator ions.","The reaction is redox."],"answer":1,"why":"The precipitation identifies Y=Mg and Z=Ca. CaSO₄ is sparingly soluble and precipitates; Mg has atomic number 12 and Ca 20. No oxidation numbers change.","tip":"Do not call an ion a spectator if it appears in the precipitate.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/15","sourceIndex":55,"stem":"Which statements about silver are correct?","context":"(1) Silver displaces copper from Cu(NO₃)₂.\n(2) Silver is obtained by strongly heating silver oxide.\n(3) Silver reacts readily with concentrated HCl to give AgCl and H₂.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"Silver is unreactive, but silver oxide is thermally unstable. Ag₂O decomposes on strong heating. Silver is below copper and hydrogen, so statements (1) and (3) are false.","tip":"Low metal reactivity corresponds to easier oxide decomposition.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/21","sourceIndex":56,"stem":"Potassium reacts completely with water containing phenolphthalein. Which observations are expected?","context":"(1) Potassium burns with a lilac flame.\n(2) The solution turns pink.\n(3) A precipitate forms after a short time.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":0,"why":"Potassium forms soluble KOH and hydrogen in a vigorous reaction. Heat may produce a lilac flame, and KOH turns phenolphthalein pink. Potassium compounds remain soluble, so no precipitate forms.","tip":"Group I hydroxides are soluble.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/30","sourceIndex":57,"stem":"For equal 100 cm³ samples of Cl₂ and CO₂ at the same conditions, which statements are correct?","context":"(1) The mass of chlorine is smaller.\n(2) The numbers of molecules are equal.\n(3) The number of atoms in chlorine is smaller.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Equal gas volumes at the same conditions contain equal molecule numbers. Cl₂ and CO₂ have equal molecule counts. Cl₂ has greater molar mass, so its mass is greater. Each Cl₂ molecule has 2 atoms versus 3 in CO₂, so chlorine has fewer atoms.","tip":"Avogadro law compares molecules; count atoms per molecule separately.","strand":""}]},{"topic":4,"name":"Acids and Bases","href":"acids-and-bases.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/15/1A/4","sourceIndex":1,"stem":"Which of the following salts CANNOT be prepared from the reaction of a metal with a dilute acid?","context":"","options":["zinc sulphate","iron(II) chloride","calcium chloride","copper(II) sulphate"],"answer":3,"why":"Choose the salt-preparation method from solubility and reactivity. D. copper(II) sulphate Copper does not react with dilute sulphuric acid.","tip":"Decide whether the product is soluble before choosing direct reaction, titration or precipitation.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/19","sourceIndex":2,"stem":"Which of the following statements about limestone is/are correct? (1) It gives a golden yellow flame in a flame test. (2) It gives a colourless gas when heated strongly. (3) It dissolves in dilute sulphuric acid to give a clear solution.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. B. (2) only (1) It gives a brick-red flame in a flame test. (3) The product CaSO₄ is insoluble in water. White precipitate is formed.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/24","sourceIndex":5,"stem":"Consider the following statements and choose the best answer: 1st statement 2nd statement All acidic gases can react with CaO(s) to form salt and water only. All acidic gases contain hydrogen as one of their constituent atoms.","context":"","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":3,"why":"Choose the salt-preparation method from solubility and reactivity. D. Both statements are false. (1) There is no water formed when acidic gases react with CaO(s). For example, CaO(s) + CO₂ (g) → CaCO₃ (s) (2) Acidic gases do not necessarily contain H atom, e.g. CO₂ , NO₂ , SO₂ .","tip":"Decide whether the product is soluble before choosing direct reaction, titration or precipitation.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/24","sourceIndex":6,"stem":"Consider the following statements and choose the best answer: 1st statement 2nd statement Iron(II) hydroxide is a base. Iron(II) hydroxide is insoluble in water.","context":"","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":1,"why":"Use the defining reactions and properties of bases and alkalis. B. Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement. Iron(II) hydroxide is a base because it neutralizes acid to give salt and water only.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/6","sourceIndex":7,"stem":"Dilute sodium hydroxide solution is added to a 0.1 M solution until in excess. Which of the following combinations is correct? Solution Observation","context":"","options":["zinc sulphate white precipitate formed","calcium nitrate white precipitate formed","lead(II) nitrate yellow precipitate formed","iron(III) sulphate dirty green precipitate formed"],"answer":1,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. B. calcium nitrate white precipitate formed A Zinc hydroxide is soluble in excess NaOH(aq). C The precipitate formed (lead(II) hydroxide) is white in colour. The precipitate is soluble in excess NaOH(aq). D The precipitate formed (iron(III) hydroxide) is yellow-brown in colour.","tip":"State both the reagent and the observable result, including colour and behaviour in excess reagent.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/9","sourceIndex":8,"stem":"Which of the following statements about potassium hydroxide solution is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["When potassium hydroxide solution is added to iron (III) sulphate solution, a dirty green precipitate is formed.","When potassium hydroxide solution is heated with ammonium chloride solution, ammonia gas is liberated.","Dilute potassium hydroxide solution contains K+ (aq) ions, H⁺ (aq) ions and OH– (aq) ions.","Concentrated potassium hydroxide solution is corrosive."],"answer":0,"why":"Use the defining reactions and properties of bases and alkalis. A. When potassium hydroxide solution is added to iron (III) sulphate solution, a dirty green precipitate is formed. The precipitate should be brown in colour.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/20","sourceIndex":9,"stem":"Aqueous calcium hydroxide can be used to (1) neutralize acidic substances in soil. (2) distinguish carbon dioxide from carbon monoxide. (3) remove sulphur dioxide from a polluted air sample.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"Apply neutralisation stoichiometry and correct titration technique. D. (1), (2) and (3) (2) Only carbon dioxide turns Ca(OH)₂ milky. (3) Ca(OH)₂ reacts with SO₂ to give CaSO₃ and H₂O","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/17","sourceIndex":10,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning NaOH(aq) and NH₃(aq) is / are correct? (1) Both of them can react with MgCl₂(aq). (2) Both of them can form a deep blue solution with Cu(OH)₂(s). (3) NaOH(aq) can react with CH₃COOH(aq), but NH₃(aq) cannot.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Test every statement against the relevant acid-base chemistry. A. (1) only (2) Cu(OH)₂ (s) does not dissolve in NaOH(aq) (3) Both NaOH(aq) and NH₃ (aq) are alkaline. They can react with CH₃COOH(aq).","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/21","sourceIndex":11,"stem":"Which of the following processes would show a blue colour? (1) adding litmus to NaOH(aq) (2) mixing CuSO₄(s) and NH₃(aq) (3) mixing K₃Fe(CN)₆(aq) and FeCl2(aq)","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"Test every statement against the relevant acid-base chemistry. D. (1), (2) and (3) (1) Litmus shows a blue colour in alkaline solution. (2) Cu²⁺ (aq) ions dissolve in excess NH₃ (aq) to give a deep blue solution. (3) K₃Fe(CN)₆ (aq) gives a blue product in the presence of Fe²⁺ (aq) ions.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/19","sourceIndex":12,"stem":"When metal X is added to copper(II) nitrate solution, a blue solid is formed as one of the products. Which of the following metals may X be? (1) zinc (2) calcium (3) sodium","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Test every statement against the relevant acid-base chemistry. D. (2) and (3) only The blue solid is probably a result of an insoluble compound containing copper(II) ions. When Ca and Na are added to Cu(NO₃)₂ (aq), they react with water in the solution and form an alkaline solution. Cu²⁺ (aq) ions react with OH⁻ (aq) ions in the alkaline solution to give a blue precipitate of Cu(OH)₂ (s).","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/19","sourceIndex":14,"stem":"In which of the following processes would a colourless gas evolve? (1) Magnesium is added to dilute sulphuric acid. (2) Ammonium chloride is heated with calcium hydroxide. (3) Water is added to a solid mixture of citric acid and sodium hydrogencarbonate.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. D. (1), (2) and (3) (1) Mg reacts with dilute H₂SO₄ (aq) to give hydrogen gas. (2) NH₄Cl reacts with Ca(OH)₂ to give ammonia gas. (3) Citric acid dissolves in water to give H⁺ (aq) which react with NaHCO₃ to give CO₂ gas.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/18","sourceIndex":15,"stem":"Which of the following pairs of substances, when mixed, would release hydrogen gas? (1) copper and concentrated HCl(aq) (2) iron and H₂SO₄(aq) (3) calcium and NaOH(aq)","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Relate acid ionisation to the observed gas or collection method. D. (2) and (3) only (1) Concentrated HCl(aq) behaves like dilute HCl(aq) (3) Calcium reacts with water in NaOH(aq) to give hydrogen gas.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/20","sourceIndex":16,"stem":"Which of the following methods can be used to distinguish between ZnCl₂(aq) and CaBr₂(aq)? (1) adding NH₃(aq) (2) performing flame test (3) evaporating to dryness","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":0,"why":"Apply solubility rules and qualitative-analysis observations. A. (1) and (2) only (1) ZnCl₂ (aq) gives a white precipitate which dissolves in excess NH₃ (aq). CaBr₂ (aq) gives no precipitate in NH₃ (aq). (2) CaBr₂ (aq) gives a brick-red flame while there is no characteristic flame colour for ZnCl₂ (aq). (3) Both ZnCl₂ (s) and CaBr₂ (s) are white solids.","tip":"State both the reagent and the observable result, including colour and behaviour in excess reagent.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/21","sourceIndex":17,"stem":"Which of the following can distinguish a sample of AgNO₃(aq) from a sample of NaNO₃(aq)? (1) adding Cu(NO₃)₂(aq) to the samples (2) adding HCl(aq) to the samples (3) adding KOH(aq) to the samples","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":2,"why":"Apply solubility rules and qualitative-analysis observations. C. (2) and (3) only (2) AgNO₃ (aq) gives a white precipitate (AgCl) in HCl(aq) while there is no observable change in NaNO₃ (aq). (3) AgNO₃ (aq) gives a brown precipitate (Ag₂O) in KOH(aq) while there is no observable change in NaNO₃ (aq).","tip":"State both the reagent and the observable result, including colour and behaviour in excess reagent.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/7","sourceIndex":18,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning aqueous ammonia is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["Aqueous ammonia is a weak alkali.","Concentrated aqueous ammonia is corrosive.","Aqueous ammonia can be used to distinguish between Pb(NO₃)₂(aq) and Al(NO₃)₃(aq).","When aqueous ammonia is added to CuSO₄(aq) until in excess, a deep blue solution is formed."],"answer":2,"why":"Use the defining reactions and properties of bases and alkalis. Aqueous ammonia cannot distinguish Pb²⁺(aq) from Al³⁺(aq): both form white hydroxide precipitates that are insoluble in excess aqueous ammonia. Therefore statement C is the incorrect statement.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/17","sourceIndex":20,"stem":"Which of the following ways is / are acceptable in the storage of the chemical concerned? (1) Store concentrated H₂SO₄(l) in a copper container. (2) Store concentrated AgNO₃(aq) in a brown glass container. (3) Store concentrated Pb(NO₃)₂(aq) in an iron container.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"Test every statement against the relevant acid-base chemistry. B. (2) only (1) Concentrated H₂SO₄ (l) reacts with unreactive metals. (2) AgNO₃ (aq) is put in a brown glass container to prevent decomposition to NO₂ (g) by sunlight. (3) Iron is more reactive than lead. Fe displaces Pb from Pb(NO₃)₂ (aq).","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/10","sourceIndex":21,"stem":"A sample of 1.02 g of potassium hydrogenphthalate (C₈H₅O₄K) is dissolved completely in distilled water, and then diluted to 250.0 cm³ . What is the concentration of the solution obtained? (Relative atomic masses: H = 1.0, C = 12.0, O = 16.0, K = 39.1)","context":"","options":["0.004 M","0.010 M","0.020 M","4.080 M"],"answer":2,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. C. 0.020 M Number of moles of C₈H₅O₄K = 1.02 12.0×8+1.0×5+16.0×4+39.1 = 4.998×10⁻³ mol Concentration of the solution = 4.998×10⁻³ ÷ 250.0 1000 = 0.020 M","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/1","sourceIndex":22,"stem":"Which of the following statements is correct?","context":"","options":["All aqueous solutions contain H⁺ (aq) ions.","The pH of all acid solutions is greater than zero.","All acidic compounds contain hydrogen as their constituent elements.","A ‘corrosive’ hazard warning label must be displayed on all reagent bottles containing acid solution."],"answer":0,"why":"Test every statement against the relevant acid-base chemistry. A. All aqueous solutions contain H⁺ (aq) ions. B Acid solutions with H⁺ concentration higher than 1 mol dm⁻³ have pH lower than zero. C CO₂ is an acidic gas. It does not contain H as its constituent element. D If the concentration of the acid solution is low, a ‘corrosive’ label is not necessary.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/6","sourceIndex":23,"stem":"The pH of a sample of sulphuric acid is 2.6. 100 cm³ of this sample is mixed with 100 cm³ of water. What is the pH of the resulting mixture?","context":"","options":["5.8","2.9","2.6","1.3"],"answer":1,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. B. 2.9 pH = –log[H⁺ (aq)] [H⁺ (aq)] = 10⁻².6 = 2.512×10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ After dilution, [H⁺ (aq)] = 2.512×10⁻³ × 100/200 = 1.256×10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ pH = –log[H⁺ (aq)] = –log(1.256×10⁻³ ) = 2.9","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/11","sourceIndex":24,"stem":"Consider the solutions W, X, Y and Z below: W 100 cm³ of 0.20 M HNO₃(aq) X 50 cm³ of 0.20 M HCl(aq) Y 100 cm³ of 0.20 M CH3CO₂H(aq) Z 50 cm³ of 0.10 M NaOH(aq) Which of the following statements is correct?","context":"","options":["The pH of Y equals –log0.2.","Mixing W and Z gives a neutral solution.","The pH of the mixture of W and X is lower than that of W.","The pH of the mixture of W and X is lower than that of the mixture of X and Y."],"answer":3,"why":"Test every statement against the relevant acid-base chemistry. D. The pH of the mixture of W and X is lower than that of the mixture of X and Y . A CH₃COOH is a weak acid. It only ionizes partially in water. [H⁺ (aq)] does not equal [CH₃COOH(aq)]. B HNO₃ + NaOH → NaNO₃ + H₂O Number of moles of H NO 3 = 0.20×50/1000 = 0.01 mol Number of moles of NaOH = 0.10×50/1000 = 0.005 mol HNO₃ is in excess. The resultant solution is acidic. C Both W and X contain a monobasic acid of same concentration. pH of mixture of W and X is the same as the pH of W and X.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/13","sourceIndex":26,"stem":"Which of the following gases, after dissolved in 1 dm³ of water, would give a solution with the highest pH?","context":"","options":["0.002 mol of NO₂","0.002 mol of SO₂","0.002 mol of NH₃","0.002 mol of HCl"],"answer":2,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. C. 0.002 mol of NH₃ NH₃ is an alkaline gas","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/18","sourceIndex":27,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning vinegar is / are correct? (1) The process of forming hydrogen ions in vinegar is reversible. (2) Neutralisation occurs when sugar is added to vinegar. (3) The pH of vinegar used in kitchen is around 1.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Apply neutralisation stoichiometry and correct titration technique. A. (1) only (2) Sugar is not a base. (3) Vinegar is not that acidic.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/2","sourceIndex":28,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning hydrochloric acid is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["It is a mineral acid.","It completely ionizes in water.","It contains aqueous hydrogen ions.","It does not contain aqueous hydroxide ions."],"answer":3,"why":"Test every statement against the relevant acid-base chemistry. D. It does not contain aqueous hydroxide ions. Water in hydrochloric acid ionizes into H⁺ and OH⁻ ions.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/4","sourceIndex":29,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning CH₃COOH and HCl is correct?","context":"","options":["CH₃COOH is a stronger acid than HCl.","The pH of 0.1 M CH₃COOH(aq) is lower than that of 0.1 M HCl(aq).","Both CH₃COOH(aq) and HCl(aq) react with NH₃(aq), each giving a salt.","Both CH₃COOH(aq) and HCl(aq) react with Ag(s), each giving a colourless gas."],"answer":2,"why":"Test every statement against the relevant acid-base chemistry. C. Both CH₃COOH(aq) and HCl(aq) react with NH₃ (aq), each giving a salt. A CH₃COOH is a weak acid while HCl is a strong acid. B CH₃COOH only slightly dissociates in water to give H⁺ while HCl completely dissociates in water. The pH of same concentration of HCl is lower than that of CH₃COOH. D Ag is an unreactive metal. It does not react with dilute acids.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/12","sourceIndex":30,"stem":"Refer to the following two solutions: X: 25.0 cm³ of 0.5 M HCl(aq) Y: 25.0 cm³ of 0.5 M CH₃COOH(aq) Which of the following statements is correct?","context":"","options":["X has a higher pH than Y.","Both X and Y have the same electrical conductivity.","The reaction of X with excess Mg(s) releases the same amount of heat as the reaction of Y with excess Mg(s).","Complete neutralisation of X and complete neutralisation of Y require the same volume of 0.5M NaOH(aq)."],"answer":3,"why":"Test every statement against the relevant acid-base chemistry. D. Complete neutralisation of X and complete neutralisation of Y require the same volume of 0.5M NaOH(aq). A CH₃COOH is a weak acid while HCl is a strong acid. Under the same concentration of acid, HCl has a lower pH as HCl ionizes completely in water to give more H⁺ ions than CH₃COOH. B A higher concentration of mobile ions in HCl(aq) results in a higher electrical conductivity. C Some of the heat given out in the reaction is used to ionize more CH₃COOH molecules to give H⁺ (aq) ions. Thus, less heat will be given out for the reaction of Y with excess Mg(s). D As both solutions contain the same volume and concentration, they have the same amount of acid molecules. One mole of the acid molecule reacts with one mole of NaOH and they require the same amount of NaOH to be neutralized.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/22","sourceIndex":31,"stem":"Both A and B are monobasic acids. The pH of 0.10 M A(aq) is 1.0 and the pH of 0.10 M B(aq) is 3.0. Which of the following statements are correct? (1) A is a stronger acid than B. (2) Some B molecules are present in B(aq). (3) Complete neutralization of 25.0 cm³ of 0.10 M A(aq) and complete neutralization of 25.0 cm³ 0.10 M B(aq) require the same number of moles of NaOH(aq).","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"Apply neutralisation stoichiometry and correct titration technique. D. (1), (2) and (3) (1) Under the same concentration and basicity, the pH of A (aq) is lower than B (aq). (3) As both solutions contain the same volume and concentration, they have the same amount of acid molecules.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/6","sourceIndex":32,"stem":"50.0 cm³ of 0.6 M FeSO₄(aq) is mixed with 150.0 cm³ of 0.2 M Fe2(SO4)3(aq). What is the concentration of SO4 2– (aq) ions in the resulting mixture?","context":"","options":["0.3 M","0.4 M","0.6 M","0.8 M"],"answer":2,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. C. 0.6 M Total number of moles of SO₄²⁻ (aq) = 0.6×50/1000 + 0.2×(150/1000)×3 = 0.12 mol Concentration of SO₄²⁻ (aq) ions = 0.12 ÷ (200/1000) = 0.6 M","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/10","sourceIndex":33,"stem":"Consider the four solutions W, X, Y and Z listed below: W: 0.01 mol dm⁻³ HNO₃(aq) X: 0.01 mol dm⁻³ H₂SO₄(aq) Y: 0.01 mol dm⁻³ KOH(aq) Z: 0.10 mol dm⁻³ KOH(aq) Which of the following represents the four solutions arranged in increasing order of pH?","context":"","options":["W, X, Y, Z","W, X, Z, Y","X, W, Y, Z","X, W, Z, Y"],"answer":2,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. C. X , W , Y , Z H₂SO₄ (aq) is a dibasic acid while HNO₃ (aq) is a monobasic acid. With the same concentration,H₂SO₄ (aq) gives more H⁺ (aq) in water. Thus H₂SO₄ (aq) has the lowest pH. KOH(aq) with a higher concentration gives more OH⁻ (aq) in water. 0.1 M has the highest pH.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/4","sourceIndex":34,"stem":"Y is a monobasic acid. The pH of 0.1 M Y(aq) is 2.91. Which of the following statements concerning the 0.1 M Y(aq) is correct?","context":"","options":["It is a concentrated solution of a weak acid.","It is a concentrated solution of a strong acid.","It is a dilute solution of a weak acid.","It is a dilute solution of a strong acid."],"answer":2,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. C. It is a dilute solution of a weak acid. For [H⁺ ] = 0.1 M, pH = –log[H⁺ ] = –log(0.1) = 1 < 2.91 Thus Y only ionizes slightly in water to give H⁺ ions. It is a weak acid.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/24","sourceIndex":35,"stem":"Consider the following statements and choose the best answer: 1st statement 2nd statement To completely neutralize 1 mole of HCl(aq), the number of moles of NH₃(aq) needed is more than the number of moles of KOH(aq) needed. NH₃(aq) is a weaker alkali than KOH(aq).","context":"","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":2,"why":"Apply neutralisation stoichiometry and correct titration technique. C. The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true. (1) 1 mole of HCl(aq) requires both NH₃ (aq) and KOH(aq) for 1 mole. NH₃ (aq) + HCl(aq) → NH₄Cl(aq) KOH(aq) + HCl(aq) → KCl(aq) + H₂O(l)","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/11","sourceIndex":36,"stem":"Which of the following pairs of aqueous solutions, when mixed, would give a precipitate?","context":"","options":["lead(II) nitrate and ammonia","copper(II) sulphate and sodium nitrate","calcium chloride and sodium nitrate","iron(II) sulphate and acidified potassium dichromate"],"answer":0,"why":"Apply solubility rules and qualitative-analysis observations. A. lead(II) nitrate and ammonia lead(II) hydroxide is formed","tip":"State both the reagent and the observable result, including colour and behaviour in excess reagent.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/2","sourceIndex":37,"stem":"A small amount of powder can dissolve in water to form a clear solution. When this solution is mixed with K2CO3(aq), a white precipitate is obtained. What can the powder be?","context":"","options":["sodium sulphate","calcium sulphate","sodium hydroxide","calcium hydroxide"],"answer":3,"why":"Apply solubility rules and qualitative-analysis observations. D. calcium hydroxide Calcium sulphate is insoluble in water. It cannot dissolve in water. All compounds containing sodium ion are soluble in water. They do not form precipitate when mixing with K 2 CO 3 (aq). Calcium carbonate is insoluble in water. When calcium hydroxide is mixed with K 2 CO 3 , CaCO₃ is formed.","tip":"State both the reagent and the observable result, including colour and behaviour in excess reagent.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/3","sourceIndex":38,"stem":"Solid W dissolves in water to form an alkaline solution. What is W?","context":"","options":["calcium oxide","calcium chloride","copper(II) oxide","copper(II) chloride"],"answer":0,"why":"Use the defining reactions and properties of bases and alkalis. A. calcium oxide Copper(II) oxide is insoluble in water. Calcium chloride and copper(II) chloride are soluble in water but the solution formed is neutral. Calcium oxide is slightly soluble in water and calcium hydroxide solution is formed.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/8","sourceIndex":39,"stem":"Which of the following pairs of substances, when mixed together, can be used to prepare copper(II) sulphate crystals?","context":"","options":["CuO(s) and H₂SO₄(aq)","CuO(s) and MgSO₄(aq)","Cu(s) and H₂SO₄(aq)","Cu(s) and MgSO₄(aq)"],"answer":0,"why":"Choose the salt-preparation method from solubility and reactivity. A. CuO(s) and H₂SO₄ (aq) Cu(s) does not react with dilute H₂SO₄ (aq) or MgSO₄ (aq). CuO(s) does not react with MgSO₄ (aq).","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/6","sourceIndex":40,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT the appropriate substance for preparing magnesium sulphate by directly mixing it with dilute sulphuric acid?","context":"","options":["magnesium metal","magnesium oxide","magnesium nitrate","magnesium carbonate"],"answer":2,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. C. magnesium nitrate Magnesium sulphate is a soluble salt. It should be prepared by reacting a metal / metal oxide / metal carbonate with an acid.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/6","sourceIndex":41,"stem":"Which of the following methods is NOT suitable for preparing zinc sulphate crystals in a school laboratory?","context":"","options":["adding excess zinc solid to copper(II) sulphate solution","adding excess zinc chloride solid to copper(II) sulphate solution","adding excess zinc oxide solid to dilute sulphuric acid","adding excess zinc carbonate solid to dilute sulphuric acid"],"answer":1,"why":"Choose the salt-preparation method from solubility and reactivity. Zinc sulphate is a soluble salt. It should be prepared by reacting a metal / metal oxide / metal carbonate with an acid. A Displacement reaction occurs. Zinc sulphate solution and copper solid will be formed.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/5","sourceIndex":42,"stem":"Which of the following is the best process to prepare lead(II) sulphate?","context":"","options":["adding lead granules to dilute sulphuric acid","adding lead(II) carbonate powder to dilute sulphuric acid","adding lead(II) chloride solid to sodium sulphate solution","adding lead(II) nitrate solution to sodium sulphate solution"],"answer":3,"why":"Choose the salt-preparation method from solubility and reactivity. D. adding lead(II) nitrate solution to sodium sulphate solution Lead(II) sulphate is an insoluble salt, which should be prepared by precipitatation. Two solutions are mixed to prepare an insoluble salt.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/8","sourceIndex":43,"stem":"Which of the following reaction routes can best be used to prepare barium sulphate from barium carbonate?","context":"","options":["BaCO3(s) H₂SO₄(aq) → BaSO₄(s)","BaCO3(s) conc. H₂SO₄(aq) → BaSO₄(s)","BaCO3(s) HCl(aq) → BaCl₂ H₂SO₄(aq) → BaSO₄(s)","BaCO3(s) conc. HCl(aq) → BaCl₂ Na₂SO₄(aq) → BaSO₄(s)"],"answer":2,"why":"Choose the salt-preparation method from solubility and reactivity. C. BaCO 3 (s) HCl(aq) → BaCl₂ H₂SO₄ (aq) → BaSO₄ (s) A & B are not workable as the reaction between BaCO 3 and H₂SO₄ gives an insoluble coat on BaCO 3 which stops further reaction. Only a small amount of BaCO 3 is produced. D is not preferred as concentrated HCl(aq) is used. Safety concerns arise.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/18","sourceIndex":44,"stem":"Which of the following steps can be involved in preparing copper(II) chloride crystals? (1) Add CuCO3(s) to HCl(aq). (2) Add Cu(NO₃)₂(s) to NaCl(aq). (3) Add Cu(s) to HCl(aq).","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Choose the salt-preparation method from solubility and reactivity. A. (1) only Copper(II) chloride is a soluble salt. It can be prepared by reacting a metal / metal oxide / metal carbonate with an acid. (3) Cu(s) is unreactive. It does not react with HCl(aq).","tip":"Decide whether the product is soluble before choosing direct reaction, titration or precipitation.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/7","sourceIndex":45,"stem":"A white solid does NOT dissolve in both water and excess aqueous ammonia. Which of the following may this solid be?","context":"","options":["Pb(NO₃)₂","Zn(OH)2","MgSO₄","CaCO₃"],"answer":3,"why":"Use the defining reactions and properties of bases and alkalis. D. CaCO₃ A&C Pb(NO₃)₂ and MgSO₄ are soluble in water. B Zn(OH) 2 dissolves in excess aqueous ammonia to give a colourless solution.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/3","sourceIndex":46,"stem":"Solid Y is soluble in cold water. When an aqueous solution of Y is added separately to sodium hydroxide solution and to acidified silver nitrate solution, a white precipitate is formed in both cases. Which of the following compounds might Y be?","context":"","options":["ammonium carbonate","zinc carbonate","lead(II) chloride","magnesium chloride"],"answer":3,"why":"Apply solubility rules and qualitative-analysis observations. D. magnesium chloride A ammonium carbonate is soluble in acidified silver nitrate solution B zinc carbonate is insoluble in water C lead(II) chloride is insoluble in cold water","tip":"State both the reagent and the observable result, including colour and behaviour in excess reagent.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/13","sourceIndex":47,"stem":"W, X, Y and Z, each represents one of the following solutions: HCl(aq) NaOH(aq)MgCl₂(aq)Na₂CO₃(aq) Given that: ‧ Mixing W and X gives a white precipitate. ‧ Mixing W and Y gives a white precipitate. ‧ Mixing W and Z gives a clear colourless solution. What is Z?","context":"","options":["HCl(aq)","NaOH(aq)","MgCl₂(aq)","Na₂CO₃(aq)"],"answer":0,"why":"Apply solubility rules and qualitative-analysis observations. A. HCl(aq) The following shows possible reactions between solutions: HCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) → NaCl(aq) + H₂O(l) HCl(aq) + Na₂CO₃ (aq) → 2NaCl(aq) + CO₂ (g) + H₂O(l) 2NaOH(aq) + MgCl₂ (aq) → Mg(OH)₂ (s) + 2NaCl(aq) Na₂CO₃ (aq) + MgCl₂ (aq) → MgCO₃ (s) + 2NaCl(aq) From the above, it can be deduced that W is MgCl₂ (aq) as MgCl₂ involves in two reactions giving precipitate. Thus Z cannot be NaOH(aq) or Na₂CO₃ (aq). It is HCl(aq).","tip":"State both the reagent and the observable result, including colour and behaviour in excess reagent.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/14","sourceIndex":48,"stem":"0.729 g of magnesium ribbon reacts with 50.0 cm³ of 0.50 M sulphuric acid. What is the mass of magnesium ribbon remained after the reaction has completed? (Relative atomic mass : Mg = 24.3)","context":"","options":["0.122 g","0.243 g","0.425 g","0.608 g"],"answer":0,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. A. 0.122 g Mg(s) + H₂SO₄ (aq) → MgSO₄ (aq) + H₂ (g) Number of moles of H₂SO₄ = 0.50 × 50.0/1000 = 0.025 mol Number of moles of Mg added = 0.729 / 24.3 = 0.03 mol Number of moles of Mg left = 0.03 – 0.025 = 0.005 mol Mass of Mg remained = 0.005 × 24.3 = 0.1215 g","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/9","sourceIndex":49,"stem":"In an experiment to prepare calcium sulphate, excess dilute sulphuric acid is added to 10.0 cm³ of 1.0 mol dm–3 calcium nitrate solution. Which of the following is the theoretical mass of the calcium sulphate obtained? (Relative atomic masses: O = 16.0, S = 32.1, Ca = 40.1)","context":"","options":["0.68 g","1.36 g","2.72 g","4.08 g"],"answer":1,"why":"Choose the salt-preparation method from solubility and reactivity. B. 1.36 g H₂SO₄ (aq) + Ca(NO 3 ) 2 (aq) → CaSO₄ (s) + 2HNO₃ (aq) 1.0 mol dm⁻³ 10.0 cm³ Number of moles of Ca(NO 3 ) 2 = 1.0×(10/1000) = 0.01 mol Number of moles of CaSO₄ = 0.01 mol Molar mass of CaSO₄ = 40.1 + 32.1 + 16.0×4 = 136.2 g mol –1 Mass of CaSO₄ = 0.01×136.2 = 1.362 g","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/6","sourceIndex":50,"stem":"Copper(II) phosphate is insoluble in water. What is the number of moles of Cu²⁺ (aq) ions remaining in the solution of the resulting mixture when 0.04 mol of CuCl₂(aq) is mixed with 0.02 mol of Na₃PO₄(aq)?","context":"","options":["0.00","0.01","0.02","0.03"],"answer":1,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. B. 0.01 3CuCl₂ (aq) + 2Na₃PO₄ (aq) →Cu₃(PO₄)₂ (s) + 6NaCl(aq) / 3Cu2 + (aq) + 2PO₄³⁻ (aq) → Cu₃(PO₄)₂ (s) Number of moles of phosphate ions = 0.02 mol From the equation, 3 moles of Cu²⁺ reacts with 2 moles of PO₄³⁻ . Number of moles of copper(II) ions consumed = 0.02 × 3 2 = 0.03 mol Number of moles of remaining copper(II) ions = 0.04 – 0.03 = 0.01 mol","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/10","sourceIndex":51,"stem":"Calcium phosphate is insoluble in water. What is the theoretical number of moles of calcium phosphate obtained when 100.0 cm³ of 0.30 mol dm–3 CaCl₂(aq) is mixed with 300.0 cm³ of 0.10 mol dm–3 Na₃PO₄(aq)? (Relative atomic masses: O = 16.0, Na = 23.0, P = 31.0, Cl = 35.5, Ca = 40.1)","context":"","options":["0.010","0.015","0.020","0.030"],"answer":0,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. A. 0.010 3CaCl₂ (aq) + 2Na₃PO₄ (aq) → Ca₃(PO₄)₂ (s) + 6NaCl(aq) 100.0 cm³ 300.0 cm³ 0.30 mol dm⁻³ 0.10 mol dm⁻³ Number of moles of CaCl₂ = 0.30×100/1000 = 0.03 mol Number of moles of Na₃PO₄ = 0.10×300/1000 = 0.03 mol CaCl₂ is the limiting reagent. Number of moles of Ca₃(PO₄)₂ formed = 0.03/3 = 0.010 mol","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/10","sourceIndex":52,"stem":"6.54 g of zinc granules are added to 100.0 cm³ of 1.0 M AgNO₃(aq). After the reaction has completed, which of the following statements is correct? (Relative atomic masses: Zn = 65.4, Ag = 107.9)","context":"","options":["Some zinc granules have reacted and no silver ions remain in the solution.","All the zinc granules have reacted and no silver ions remain in the solution.","All the zinc granules have reacted and some silver ions remain in the solution.","The mass of the zinc granules reacted is equal to the mass of the solid product formed."],"answer":0,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. A. Some zinc granules have reacted and no silver ions remain in the solution. Zn(s) + 2AgNO₃ (aq) → Zn(NO₃)₂ (aq) + 2Ag(s) Number of moles of Zn = 6.54 / 65.4 = 0.1 mol Number of moles of AgNO₃ = 1.0 × 100.0/1000 = 0.1 mol According to the equation, 1 mole of Zn reacts with 2 moles of AgNO₃ . Thus AgNO₃ is the limiting reagent.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/5","sourceIndex":53,"stem":"25.00 cm³ of 0.50 M lead(II) nitrate solution is mixed with 50.00 cm³ of 1.00 M sodium chloride solution. Insoluble lead(II) chloride is formed during mixing. What is the concentration of Cl– (aq) in the mixture?","context":"","options":["0.33 M","0.50 M","0.75 M","1.50 M"],"answer":0,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. A. 0.33 M Pb²⁺ (aq) + 2Cl⁻ (aq) → PbCl₂ (s) Number of moles of Pb²⁺ present = 0.50×(25/1000) = 0.0125 mol Number of moles of Cl⁻ present = 1.00×(50/1000) = 0.05mol As the mole ratio of Pb²⁺ to Cl⁻ is 1 to 2, Pb²⁺ (aq) is the limiting reagent. Number of moles of Cl⁻ (aq) left = 0.05 – 2(0.0125) = 0.025 mol Volume of the mixture = 25.00 + 50.00 = 75.00 cm³ Concentration of Cl⁻ (aq) = 0.025 / (75/1000) = 0.33 M","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/5","sourceIndex":54,"stem":"15.0 cm³ of 0.20 M Ba(NO₃)₂(aq) is added to 25.0 cm³ of 0.10 M Na₂SO₄(aq). After the reaction is completed, which of the following ions has the highest concentration in the mixture?","context":"","options":["SO4 2– (aq)","NO3 – (aq)","Ba²⁺ (aq)","Na+ (aq)"],"answer":1,"why":"Use the correct acid-base relationship, mole ratio and units. B. NO₃⁻ (aq) Ba(NO₃)₂ (aq) + Na₂SO₄ (aq) → BaSO₄ (s) + 2NaNO₃ (aq) / Ba²⁺ (aq) + SO₄²⁻ (aq) → BaSO₄ (s) Ion Number of moles added (mol) Number of moles reacted (mol) Concentration in the mixture (M) SO₄²⁻ (aq) 0.10×25/1000 = 0.0025 0.0025 0 NO₃⁻ (aq) 2×0.20×15/1000 = 0.006 / 0.006 ÷ [(15+25)/1000] = 0.15 Ba²⁺ (aq) 0.20×15/1000 = 0.003 0.0025 (0.003 – 0.0025) ÷ (40/1000) = 0.0125 Na⁺ (aq) 2×0.10×25/1000 = 0.005 / 0.005 ÷ (40/1000) = 0.125","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/22","sourceIndex":55,"stem":"Consider the following two solutions: Solution A : 100 cm³ of 1.0 M nitric acid Solution B : 100 cm³ of 0.5 M sulphuric acid Which of the following statements are correct? (1) Both solution A and solution B can react with iron. (2) Complete neutralisation of solution A and complete neutralisation of solution B require the same volume of 1.0 M potassium hydroxide solution. (3) When 0.05 mol of CaCO₃(s) is separately added to solution A and to solution B, the same number of moles of gas is evolved in both cases.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":0,"why":"Apply neutralisation stoichiometry and correct titration technique. A. (1) and (2) only (3) CaCO₃ reacts with H₂SO₄ to give insoluble CaSO₄ . The CaSO₄ formed prevents further reaction of CaCO₃ with H₂SO₄ . The number of moles of gas evolved is smaller in this case.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/8","sourceIndex":56,"stem":"In an experiment, 25.0 cm³ of HCl(aq) is measured with apparatus X and is placed in apparatus Y. The HCl(aq) in Y is then titrated with a standard NaOH(aq). Which of the following combinations is correct? X Y","context":"","options":["measuring cylinder beaker","measuring cylinder conical flask","pipette beaker","pipette conical flask"],"answer":3,"why":"Test every statement against the relevant acid-base chemistry. A pipette measures and transfers the fixed 25.0 cm³ portion accurately. The acid is placed in a conical flask so the mixture can be swirled safely during titration. Therefore X is a pipette and Y is a conical flask.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/4","sourceIndex":59,"stem":"Which of the following is an INCORRECT procedure in titration?","context":"","options":["Rinse the pipette with the solution to be delivered before titration.","Rinse the conical flask with the solution to be held before titration.","Take the burette readings with eyes on the same level as the meniscus.","Make sure that there are no air bubbles in the burette filled with the titrant."],"answer":1,"why":"Apply neutralisation stoichiometry and correct titration technique. B. Rinse the conical flask with the solution to be held before titration. This increases the actual amount of the chemical species added as some of the solution will remain on the inner wall of the conical flask.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/10","sourceIndex":60,"stem":"12.80 cm³ of a H₂SO₄(aq) sample is completely neutralised by 25.0 cm³ of 0.10 M KOH(aq). What is the concentration of the H₂SO₄(aq) sample?","context":"","options":["2.56 × 10⁻² M","9.77 × 10⁻² M","1.95 × 10⁻¹ M","3.91 × 10⁻¹ M"],"answer":1,"why":"Apply neutralisation stoichiometry and correct titration technique. H₂SO₄(aq) + 2KOH(aq) → K₂SO₄(aq) + 2H₂O(l). Moles of KOH = 0.100 × 25.0/1000 = 2.50 × 10⁻³ mol, so moles of H₂SO₄ = 1.25 × 10⁻³ mol. Concentration = 1.25 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.01280 = 9.77 × 10⁻² M.","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/16","sourceIndex":61,"stem":"A sample of sulphuric acid was completely neutralized by 25.0 cm³ of 0.200 M potassium hydroxide solution. The salt solution obtained was then made up to 100.0 cm³ with deionized water. What is the concentration of the resulting salt solution?","context":"","options":["0.0125 M","0.0250 M","0.0375 M","0.0500 M"],"answer":1,"why":"Apply neutralisation stoichiometry and correct titration technique. B. 0.0250 M H₂SO₄ (aq) + 2KOH(aq) → K₂SO₄ (aq) + 2H₂O(l) Number of moles of KOH = 0.2 × 25/1000 = 0.005 mol Number of moles of K₂SO₄ = 0.005 ÷ 2 = 0.0025 mol Concentration = 0.0025 ÷ (100/1000) = 0.025 M","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/4","sourceIndex":62,"stem":"25.00 cm³ of 0.051 M C₄H₄O₄(aq) can completely neutralize 22.18 cm³ of 0.115 M KOH(aq). What is the basicity of the acid C₄H₄O₄?","context":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":1,"why":"Apply neutralisation stoichiometry and correct titration technique. B. 2 H n X(aq) + n KOH(aq) → K n X(aq) + n H₂O(l) Number of moles of KOH = 0.115 × 22.18/1000 = 2.5507×10⁻³ mol Number of moles of C₄H₄O₄ = 0.051 × 25.00/1000 = 1.275×10⁻³ mol n = 2.5507×10⁻³ / 1.275×10⁻³ = 2","tip":"Write the balanced equation first, convert all volumes to dm³, and check the limiting amount before selecting an option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/7","sourceIndex":64,"stem":"Which of the following pairs of aqueous solutions, upon mixing, would have the lowest electrical conductivity?","context":"","options":["20.0 cm³ of 0.1 M HNO₃ and 20.0 cm³ of 0.1 M KOH","20.0 cm³ of 0.1 M H₂SO₄ and 20.0 cm³ of 0.1 M Ba(OH)₂","20.0 cm³ of 0.1 M CH₃COOH and 20.0 cm³ of 0.1 M NH₃","20.0 cm³ of 0.1 M HCl and 20.0 cm³ of 0.1 M C₆H₁₂O₆(glucose)"],"answer":1,"why":"Test every statement against the relevant acid-base chemistry. Equal volumes of H₂SO₄(aq) and Ba(OH)₂(aq) react to form water and insoluble BaSO₄(s). Neutralisation removes H⁺ and OH⁻, while precipitation removes Ba²⁺ and SO₄²⁻, leaving the fewest mobile ions and therefore the lowest electrical conductivity.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/17","sourceIndex":67,"stem":"Which methods can separate Cu²⁺ from an aqueous solution containing Cu²⁺ and Al³⁺? (1) heating the mixture (2) adding excess NaOH(aq) to the mixture (3) adding Na₂SO₄(aq) to the mixture","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"Apply solubility rules and qualitative-analysis observations. Excess NaOH(aq) precipitates both hydroxides initially, but amphoteric Al(OH)₃ dissolves in excess NaOH while Cu(OH)₂ remains as a solid. Filtering therefore separates Cu²⁺ as copper(II) hydroxide.","tip":"Check every word in each statement; one incorrect condition is enough to reject the whole option.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/24","sourceIndex":68,"stem":"Consider the following statements and choose the best answer: 1st statement 2nd statement Concentrated hydrochloric acid and concentrated nitric acid cannot be distinguished by adding zinc metal to them. Both hydrochloric acid and nitric acid are strong acids.","context":"","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":2,"why":"Apply solubility rules and qualitative-analysis observations. Zinc gives different observations with concentrated hydrochloric acid and concentrated nitric acid because concentrated nitric acid is oxidising. Both acids are strong, but their acid strength does not explain the different redox behaviour.","tip":"State both the reagent and the observable result, including colour and behaviour in excess reagent.","strand":""}]},{"topic":5,"name":"Fossil Fuels and Carbon","href":"fossil-fuels-carbon.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/16/1A/17","sourceIndex":0,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning petroleum is/are correct?","context":"(1) It is a source of aliphatic hydrocarbons.\n(2) It can be separated into liquids of different viscosity by a separating funnel.\n(3) It is a fossil fuel derived from ancient marine organisms.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":2,"why":"Petroleum is a fossil source of aliphatic hydrocarbons. Statements (1) and (3) are correct. Petroleum fractions are separated by fractional distillation according to boiling ranges, not by a separating funnel.","tip":"","strand":"Petroleum"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/10","sourceIndex":3,"stem":"Petroleum is separated by fractional distillation. Fraction U is obtained from the upper part of the tower and fraction L from the lower part. Which statement is correct?","context":"","options":["U has a higher boiling range and a higher viscosity than L.","U has a higher boiling range and a lower viscosity than L.","U has a lower boiling range and a higher viscosity than L.","U has a lower boiling range and a lower viscosity than L."],"answer":3,"why":"An upper fraction has a lower boiling range and lower viscosity. Molecules in fraction U are generally smaller than those in fraction L. Their intermolecular attractions are weaker, so U boils at lower temperatures and flows more readily.","tip":"","strand":"Petroleum fractions"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/2","sourceIndex":4,"stem":"Which of the following gases can cause acid rain?","context":"","options":["CH₄(g)","CO(g)","N₂(g)","SO₂(g)"],"answer":3,"why":"Sulphur dioxide contributes to acid rain. SO₂ dissolves and is oxidised in atmospheric water to form acidic solutions. The other listed gases do not cause acid rain in this context.","tip":"","strand":"Air pollution"},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/24","sourceIndex":5,"stem":"Consider the two statements and choose the best answer.","context":"1st statement Burning fossil fuels can cause acid rain.\n2nd statement Burning fossil fuels produces carbon dioxide.","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement correctly explains the 1st.","Both statements are true but the 2nd statement does NOT correctly explain the 1st.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":1,"why":"Both statements are true, but carbon dioxide is not the acid-rain explanation. Acid rain is mainly associated with sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides. Carbon dioxide from combustion contributes to the greenhouse effect, not the stated acid-rain cause.","tip":"","strand":"Environmental impact"},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/22","sourceIndex":6,"stem":"Which statements concerning burning coal under room conditions are correct?","context":"(1) Burning coal forms both acidic and non-acidic substances.\n(2) Burning coal forms both gaseous and non-gaseous substances.\n(3) Burning coal forms both poisonous and non-poisonous substances.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"All three descriptions are correct. Coal combustion can form acidic oxides and non-acidic products, gases and particulates/ash, and poisonous pollutants alongside non-poisonous products.","tip":"","strand":"Combustion of coal"},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/17","sourceIndex":7,"stem":"What are the advantages of using natural gas rather than coal as a fuel in power stations?","context":"(1) Compared with coal, natural gas burns more completely.\n(2) Compared with coal, natural gas contains fewer sulphur-containing substances.\n(3) Natural gas is a renewable energy source, but coal is not.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":0,"why":"Natural gas burns more cleanly but is not renewable. Natural gas mixes readily with air and usually burns more completely. It contains less sulphur than coal. Both natural gas and coal are fossil fuels and are non-renewable.","tip":"","strand":"Natural gas"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/10","sourceIndex":8,"stem":"Which pollutant CANNOT be converted into less harmful substances by a catalytic converter?","context":"","options":["nitrogen oxides","sulphur dioxide","carbon monoxide","unburnt hydrocarbons"],"answer":1,"why":"A catalytic converter does not remove sulphur dioxide. Catalytic converters reduce nitrogen oxides and oxidise carbon monoxide and unburnt hydrocarbons. Sulphur dioxide requires a different removal method, such as flue-gas desulphurisation.","tip":"","strand":"Catalytic converters"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/9","sourceIndex":9,"stem":"Coal-fired power-plant gases contain SO₂. What is the most suitable method for removing SO₂ before discharge?","context":"","options":["Pass the gases through calcium oxide.","Pass the gases through concentrated sulphuric acid.","Cool the gases to liquefy SO₂.","Pass the gases through an organic solvent such as hexane."],"answer":0,"why":"Basic calcium oxide removes acidic sulphur dioxide. Calcium oxide reacts with SO₂ to form a solid sulphite/sulphate product. The other methods are ineffective or impractical for flue-gas treatment.","tip":"","strand":"Flue-gas treatment"},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/7","sourceIndex":10,"stem":"Which air pollutant can be removed by electrostatic precipitators in coal-fired power plants?","context":"","options":["suspended particulates","carbon monoxide","nitrogen oxides","sulphur dioxide"],"answer":0,"why":"Electrostatic precipitators remove suspended solid particles. Particles are electrically charged and attracted to oppositely charged collecting plates. The device does not chemically remove gaseous CO, NOₓ or SO₂.","tip":"","strand":"Electrostatic precipitators"},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/22","sourceIndex":11,"stem":"Which measures can reduce air-pollutant emissions from the combustion of fossil fuels?","context":"(1) installing scrubbers in coal-fired power plants\n(2) installing catalytic converters in petrol-driven cars\n(3) installing electrostatic precipitators in coal-fired power plants","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"All three pollution-control measures are effective. Scrubbers remove acidic gases, catalytic converters treat selected vehicle-exhaust pollutants, and electrostatic precipitators collect suspended particles.","tip":"","strand":"Pollution control"},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/20","sourceIndex":12,"stem":"Which characteristics are exhibited by members of a homologous series?","context":"(1) They have similar chemical properties.\n(2) They display a gradation in physical properties.\n(3) They can be represented by the same general formula.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"All three are defining characteristics of a homologous series. Members share a functional group and general formula, react similarly, and show gradual changes in physical properties with molecular size.","tip":"","strand":"Homologous series"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/19","sourceIndex":13,"stem":"Members of the homologous series of alkenes have the same","context":"(1) structural formula.\n(2) empirical formula.\n(3) general formula.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (2) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Alkenes share the empirical formula CH₂ and general formula CₙH₂ₙ. Their structural formulae differ because their chain lengths differ. For ordinary mono-alkenes, CₙH₂ₙ simplifies to the same empirical formula CH₂.","tip":"","strand":"Alkenes"},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/10","sourceIndex":14,"stem":"Which of the following gases is NOT a mixture?","context":"","options":["town gas","natural gas","butane gas","refinery gas"],"answer":2,"why":"Butane gas is treated as a pure substance. Town gas, natural gas and refinery gas contain mixtures of gaseous substances. Butane gas consists of butane molecules.","tip":"","strand":"Fuel gases"},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/8","sourceIndex":15,"stem":"Which molecular formula can represent an alkanoic acid?","context":"","options":["CH₂O","C₂H₆O₂","C₄H₈O₂","C₄H₁₀O₂"],"answer":2,"why":"C₄H₈O₂ matches the alkanoic-acid formula CₙH₂ₙO₂. For n = 4, the saturated monocarboxylic-acid formula is C₄H₈O₂. The other formulae do not fit CₙH₂ₙO₂.","tip":"","strand":"Alkanoic acids"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/23","sourceIndex":16,"stem":"Which statements concerning ethanoic acid are correct?","context":"(1) Ethanoic acid is present in vinegar.\n(2) Ethanoic acid changes phenolphthalein indicator from colourless to pink.\n(3) Ethanoic acid is an alkanoic acid.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":1,"why":"Ethanoic acid is present in vinegar and is an alkanoic acid. Phenolphthalein remains colourless in acidic ethanoic-acid solution; it turns pink in alkaline solution.","tip":"","strand":"Ethanoic acid"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/17","sourceIndex":17,"stem":"What is the systematic name of CH₂BrCHBrCH₂CH₂I?","context":"","options":["1-iodo-3,4-dibromobutane","4-iodo-1,2-dibromobutane","1,2-dibromo-4-iodobutane","3,4-dibromo-1-iodobutane"],"answer":2,"why":"The name is 1,2-dibromo-4-iodobutane. Numbering from the bromo end gives the lower set of locants, 1,2,4 rather than 1,3,4. Substituent names are then listed alphabetically: bromo before iodo.","tip":"","strand":"Systematic naming"},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/5","sourceIndex":18,"stem":"What is the systematic name of Cl₂CH–CH=CH–CH=CH₂?","context":"","options":["1-dichloropenta-2,4-diene","5,5-chloropenta-1,3-diene","1,1-dichloropenta-2,4-diene","5,5-dichloropenta-1,3-diene"],"answer":3,"why":"The name is 5,5-dichloropenta-1,3-diene. The chain is numbered to give the two double bonds the lowest locants, 1 and 3. Both chlorine substituents are then at carbon 5.","tip":"","strand":"Systematic naming"},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/11","sourceIndex":19,"stem":"Compound X has the structure CH₂=CHCH₂OH. What is its systematic name?","context":"","options":["prop-1-en-3-ol","prop-2-en-1-ol","3-hydroxypropene","1-hydroxyprop-3-ene"],"answer":1,"why":"The name is prop-2-en-1-ol. The hydroxyl group is the principal functional group and receives the lowest locant. Numbering from the OH end places OH at carbon 1 and the double bond at carbon 2.","tip":"","strand":"Systematic naming"},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/11","sourceIndex":20,"stem":"What is the systematic name of (CH₃)₂CHCH₂C(OH)(CH₃)CH₂CH=CHCH₃?","context":"","options":["5,7-dimethyloct-2-en-5-ol","2,4-dimethyloct-6-en-4-ol","7,7-dimethylhept-2-en-5-ol","1,1-dimethylhept-5-en-3-ol"],"answer":1,"why":"The name is 2,4-dimethyloct-6-en-4-ol. The longest chain containing both the OH group and C=C bond has eight carbons. Numbering gives the OH group locant 4 rather than 5, with methyl groups at 2 and 4 and the double bond at 6.","tip":"","strand":"Systematic naming"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/9","sourceIndex":22,"stem":"One mole of a hydrocarbon requires 9 mol of oxygen for complete combustion. Which hydrocarbon may it be?","context":"","options":["C₆H₆","C₆H₁₀","C₆H₁₂","C₆H₁₄"],"answer":2,"why":"C₆H₁₂ requires 9 mol of O₂ per mole. For CₓHᵧ, the oxygen requirement is x + y/4. For C₆H₁₂ this is 6 + 12/4 = 9.","tip":"","strand":"Combustion"},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/10","sourceIndex":24,"stem":"One mole of methane reacts with two moles of chlorine in the presence of light. Which best describes the organic products formed?","context":"","options":["one mole of CCl₄","one mole of CH₂Cl₂","a mixture containing only CCl₄ and CH₂Cl₂","a mixture containing CH₃Cl, CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃ and CCl₄"],"answer":3,"why":"Successive substitution produces a mixture of chlorinated methanes. Substitution does not stop cleanly after two replacements. CH₃Cl, CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃ and CCl₄ can all form in the reaction mixture.","tip":"","strand":"Free-radical substitution"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/11","sourceIndex":25,"stem":"The monosubstitution of methane with chlorine under diffuse sunlight involves several steps. Which step initiates the reaction?","context":"","options":["Cl₂ → 2Cl•","CH₄ → CH₃• + H•","CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl","CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + H• + Cl•"],"answer":0,"why":"Initiation is homolytic fission of chlorine. Diffuse light supplies energy to split Cl₂ evenly, producing two chlorine radicals that start the chain reaction.","tip":"","strand":"Free-radical substitution"},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/17","sourceIndex":27,"stem":"The monosubstitution of CH₄ with Cl₂ under diffuse sunlight involves initiation, propagation and termination. Which listed steps can terminate the reaction?","context":"(1) Cl₂ → Cl• + Cl•\n(2) CH₃• + Cl• → CH₃Cl\n(3) CH₄ + Cl• → CH₃• + HCl","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"Only the radical-combination step is termination. Step (2) consumes two radicals and produces no new radical. Step (1) creates radicals and is initiation; step (3) is propagation.","tip":"","strand":"Free-radical substitution"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/9","sourceIndex":29,"stem":"Which equation can represent cracking of C₁₆H₃₄ to give propene and other organic products?","context":"","options":["C₁₆H₃₄ → C₂H₄ + C₇H₁₄ + C₇H₁₆","C₁₆H₃₄ → C₃H₆ + C₆H₁₂ + C₇H₁₄","C₁₆H₃₄ → C₃H₆ + C₆H₁₄ + C₇H₁₄","C₁₆H₃₄ → C₃H₈ + C₅H₁₀ + C₈H₁₆"],"answer":2,"why":"Option C is atom-balanced and includes propene. The products in C contain 16 carbon atoms and 34 hydrogen atoms in total: 3 + 6 + 7 = 16 and 6 + 14 + 14 = 34.","tip":"","strand":"Cracking equations"},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/5","sourceIndex":41,"stem":"On cracking, one molecule of C₂₃H₄₈ gives one molecule of ethene, one molecule of propane and two molecules of hydrocarbon X. What is the molecular formula of X?","context":"","options":["C₉H₁₆","C₉H₁₈","C₁₈H₃₄","C₁₈H₃₆"],"answer":1,"why":"X has formula C₉H₁₈. After subtracting C₂H₄ and C₃H₈ from C₂₃H₄₈, the two X molecules together contain C₁₈H₃₆. Each X is therefore C₉H₁₈.","tip":"","strand":"Cracking and atom balance"},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/9","sourceIndex":43,"stem":"Which step occurs in the propagation stage of methane monosubstitution with chlorine under diffuse sunlight?","context":"","options":["Cl₂ → 2Cl•","CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl","Cl• + CH₄ → CH₃• + HCl","Cl• + CH₃• → CH₃Cl"],"answer":2,"why":"A chlorine radical abstracts hydrogen during propagation. Cl• reacts with CH₄ to form HCl and CH₃•. A radical is consumed and another radical is produced, allowing the chain to continue.","tip":"","strand":"Free-radical substitution"},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/22","sourceIndex":44,"stem":"P and Q are liquid petroleum fractions. The boiling range of P is higher than that of Q. Which statements are correct?","context":"(1) The colour of P is darker than that of Q.\n(2) The viscosity of P is higher than that of Q.\n(3) The ease of ignition of P is higher than that of Q.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":0,"why":"P is darker and more viscous, but less easily ignited. A higher-boiling fraction contains larger hydrocarbons. It is generally darker and more viscous, with lower volatility and therefore lower ease of ignition.","tip":"","strand":"Petroleum fractions"}]},{"topic":6,"name":"Microscopic World II","href":"microscopic-world-ii.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/13/1A/23","sourceIndex":0,"stem":"To which of the following molecules is/are the ‘octet rule’ NOT applicable?","context":"(1) OF₂\n(2) NO₂\n(3) CS₂","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"Only NO₂ has a non-octet structure. OF₂ and CS₂ can be represented with eight outer-shell electrons around their central atoms. NO₂ has an odd total number of valence electrons, so one electron remains unpaired and a complete octet cannot be assigned to every atom.","tip":"An odd total number of valence electrons is an immediate warning that the octet rule cannot be satisfied everywhere.","strand":"Octet rule"},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/22","sourceIndex":1,"stem":"Which of the following molecules have non-octet structures?","context":"(1) NO₂\n(2) PBr₃\n(3) BCl₃","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":1,"why":"NO₂ and BCl₃ are non-octet molecules. NO₂ has an odd number of valence electrons. In BCl₃, boron has only six electrons around it. PBr₃ has three P–Br bonds and one lone pair on phosphorus, giving phosphorus an octet.","tip":"Check for three common exceptions: odd-electron species, electron-deficient central atoms, and expanded octets.","strand":"Non-octet structures"},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/12","sourceIndex":5,"stem":"Which of the following molecules is planar?","context":"","options":["BF₃","NH₃","CH₄","PCl₅"],"answer":0,"why":"BF₃ is trigonal planar. BF₃ has three bonding pairs and no lone pair around boron, so all four atoms lie in one plane. NH₃ is trigonal pyramidal, CH₄ is tetrahedral and PCl₅ is trigonal bipyramidal.","tip":"A trigonal planar molecule is planar; a lone pair often changes a trigonal arrangement into a pyramid.","strand":"Molecular shape"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/21","sourceIndex":7,"stem":"Which of the following molecules have a similar shape?","context":"(1) BCl₃\n(2) NH₃\n(3) PF₃","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":2,"why":"NH₃ and PF₃ are both trigonal pyramidal. BCl₃ has three bond pairs and no lone pair, giving a trigonal planar shape. NH₃ and PF₃ each have three bond pairs and one lone pair, giving trigonal pyramidal shapes.","tip":"Similar formula patterns do not guarantee similar shapes; count lone pairs on the central atom.","strand":"Molecular shape"},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/12","sourceIndex":8,"stem":"Which of the following molecules is polar?","context":"","options":["CO₂","PCl₃","SiF₄","SF₆"],"answer":1,"why":"PCl₃ is polar because its bond dipoles do not cancel. PCl₃ is trigonal pyramidal and asymmetric, so it has a net dipole. CO₂ is linear, SiF₄ is tetrahedral and SF₆ is octahedral; their identical surrounding atoms make the bond dipoles cancel.","tip":"A molecule can contain polar bonds but still be non-polar if its bond dipoles cancel by symmetry.","strand":"Molecular polarity"},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/16","sourceIndex":9,"stem":"Which of the following molecules is/are non-polar?","context":"(1) BCl₃\n(2) PCl₃\n(3) CHCl₃","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Only BCl₃ is non-polar. BCl₃ is trigonal planar and symmetric, so its B–Cl bond dipoles cancel. PCl₃ is trigonal pyramidal, and CHCl₃ is tetrahedral with different surrounding atoms; both have net dipoles.","tip":"Test symmetry using both the molecular shape and whether the surrounding atoms are identical.","strand":"Molecular polarity"},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/23","sourceIndex":10,"stem":"When a negatively charged rod is placed near a jet of liquid running out from a burette, the jet of liquid deflects towards the rod. Which of the following may the liquid be?","context":"(1) water\n(2) hexane\n(3) trichloromethane","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":1,"why":"Water and trichloromethane are polar liquids. A charged rod attracts the oppositely oriented side of polar molecules, deflecting the jet. Water and trichloromethane are polar; hexane is non-polar.","tip":"Deflection by a charged rod is evidence of permanent molecular polarity.","strand":"Polar molecules"},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/11","sourceIndex":11,"stem":"Which of the following statements about intermolecular forces is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["Hydrogen bonding is an electrostatic attraction.","Van der Waals’ forces exist between polar molecules and also between non-polar molecules.","The intermolecular forces in bromine are stronger than those in chlorine.","The intermolecular forces in hydrogen chloride are stronger than those in hydrogen fluoride."],"answer":3,"why":"HF has stronger intermolecular attraction than HCl. Hydrogen fluoride molecules form strong hydrogen bonds. Hydrogen chloride molecules do not; they are held mainly by dipole–dipole and van der Waals attractions. Therefore the final statement is incorrect.","tip":"Do not compare molecular size alone when one substance can form hydrogen bonds.","strand":"Intermolecular forces"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/10","sourceIndex":12,"stem":"Which of the following processes involves the breaking of hydrogen bonds?","context":"","options":["H₂(l) → H₂(g)","HBr(l) → HBr(g)","CH₃OH(l) → CH₃OH(g)","CH₃CHO(l) → CH₃CHO(g)"],"answer":2,"why":"Methanol molecules form hydrogen bonds. CH₃OH contains an O–H bond, so its molecules form hydrogen bonds that are overcome during vaporisation. H₂, HBr and CH₃CHO do not have hydrogen directly bonded to N, O or F.","tip":"An electronegative atom is not enough; hydrogen must be directly bonded to N, O or F.","strand":"Hydrogen bonding"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/24","sourceIndex":13,"stem":"Consider the two statements and choose the best answer.","context":"1st statement Butane and methylpropane have the same physical properties.\n2nd statement Butane and methylpropane have the same molecular formula.","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":2,"why":"The isomers have the same formula but different physical properties. Both compounds have molecular formula C₄H₁₀. Their shapes and surface contact differ, producing different van der Waals attractions and hence different melting and boiling points.","tip":"Same molecular formula identifies isomers; it does not mean identical physical properties.","strand":"Structure and properties"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/16","sourceIndex":14,"stem":"Which of the following compounds has the highest boiling point?","context":"","options":["HF","HCl","PH₃","H₂Se"],"answer":0,"why":"HF has the highest boiling point because it forms hydrogen bonds. Only HF has hydrogen directly bonded to a highly electronegative F atom, so strong intermolecular hydrogen bonds form. The other compounds rely on weaker intermolecular attractions.","tip":"Check hydrogen bonding before comparing van der Waals forces by molecular size.","strand":"Hydrogen bonding"},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/24","sourceIndex":15,"stem":"Consider the two statements and choose the best answer.","context":"1st statement The boiling point of hydrogen chloride is higher than that of hydrogen fluoride.\n2nd statement The molecular size of hydrogen chloride is greater than that of hydrogen fluoride.","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":2,"why":"HF boils higher despite being smaller because it forms hydrogen bonds. The first statement is false: hydrogen fluoride has the higher boiling point because HF molecules form hydrogen bonds. The second statement is true: HCl is the larger molecule.","tip":"A special intermolecular force such as hydrogen bonding can outweigh a molecular-size trend.","strand":"Boiling point"},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/24","sourceIndex":16,"stem":"Consider the two statements and choose the best answer.","context":"1st statement Hydrogen iodide has a higher boiling point than hydrogen bromide.\n2nd statement The van der Waals’ forces between hydrogen iodide molecules are stronger than those between hydrogen bromide molecules.","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":0,"why":"The larger HI molecule has stronger van der Waals forces. HI has more electrons and a more polarisable electron cloud than HBr. Its stronger van der Waals attraction requires more energy to overcome, so HI has the higher boiling point. Both statements are true and causally linked.","tip":"Within a related molecular series without hydrogen bonding, larger electron clouds usually give higher boiling points.","strand":"Van der Waals forces"},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/22","sourceIndex":18,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning ice and water at 0 °C are correct?","context":"(1) The density of ice is lower than that of water because ice has an open structure but water does not.\n(2) In ice, the hydrogen bonds between the molecules are weaker than the covalent bonds in the molecules.\n(3) In ice, each molecule links up with only two neighbouring molecules by hydrogen bonds.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":0,"why":"Statements (1) and (2) are correct. Ice has an open hydrogen-bonded lattice, so the molecules are farther apart and ice is less dense than water. Hydrogen bonds are weaker than the O–H covalent bonds within each molecule. In ice, each water molecule forms hydrogen bonds in a tetrahedral arrangement with four neighbours, not two.","tip":"Do not confuse the two O–H covalent bonds inside H₂O with the four neighbouring molecules in the ice lattice.","strand":"Ice structure"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/17","sourceIndex":19,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning ice and water is/are correct?","context":"(1) Ice has an open structure but water does not.\n(2) The number of hydrogen bonds formed between H₂O molecules in ice is double that in water.\n(3) The shape of an H₂O molecule in ice is tetrahedral while the shape of an H₂O molecule in water is V-shaped.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Only the open structure statement is correct. Ice has an open lattice; liquid water does not. The marking source treats the total number of hydrogen bonds as unchanged in this comparison. The shape of an individual H₂O molecule remains V-shaped in both states; tetrahedral describes the hydrogen-bond arrangement around a molecule in ice.","tip":"Distinguish molecular shape from the arrangement of neighbouring molecules in a lattice.","strand":"Ice and water"},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/17","sourceIndex":20,"stem":"Which of the following statements is/are correct?","context":"(1) The density of H₂O(l) is lower than that of H₂O(g).\n(2) When ice changes to water, the open structure of ice collapses.\n(3) When the temperature of water rises from 10 °C to 30 °C, the average distance between H₂O molecules increases.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Statements (2) and (3) are correct. Liquid water is much denser than water vapour, so statement (1) is false. On melting, the open ice lattice collapses. From 10 °C to 30 °C, thermal expansion increases the average separation between water molecules.","tip":"For density comparisons, first identify the physical states; gas particles are much farther apart.","strand":"Density and temperature"},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/24","sourceIndex":21,"stem":"Consider the two statements and choose the best answer.","context":"1st statement The boiling point of H₂O is lower than that of HF.\n2nd statement The electronegativity of oxygen is lower than that of fluorine.","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":2,"why":"Water boils higher; oxygen is less electronegative than fluorine. The first statement is false because water forms a more extensive hydrogen-bonded network than HF, giving water the higher boiling point. The second statement is true: fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen.","tip":"Boiling point depends on the extent and strength of intermolecular bonding, not electronegativity alone.","strand":"Hydrogen bonding"},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/18","sourceIndex":22,"stem":"In which of the following compounds does hydrogen bonding exist?","context":"(1) CH₃F(l)\n(2) CH₃CH₂NH₂(l)\n(3) HCHO(l)","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"Only ethylamine forms intermolecular hydrogen bonds. CH₃CH₂NH₂ contains N–H bonds, so its molecules form hydrogen bonds. CH₃F has no H–F bond, and HCHO has no O–H bond.","tip":"Hydrogen bonding requires H to be directly bonded to N, O or F. This 2026 explanation is tutor-prepared from the supplied question and answer key.","strand":"Hydrogen bonding"}]},{"topic":7,"name":"Redox, Cells and Electrolysis","href":"redox-reactions-cells-electrolysis.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/12/1A/6","sourceIndex":0,"stem":"What is the oxidation number of Cu in Cu(NH₃)₄Cl₂?","context":"","options":["0","+2","+4","+6"],"answer":1,"why":"Copper has oxidation number +2. Let the oxidation number of Cu be x. The NH₃ ligands are neutral and each chloride is −1: x + 4(0) + 2(−1) = 0, hence x = +2.","tip":"Set the sum of oxidation numbers equal to the overall charge.","strand":"Oxidation numbers"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/7","sourceIndex":1,"stem":"The oxidation number of Pb in Pb₁₀(VO₄)₆F₂ is +2. What is the oxidation number of V?","context":"","options":["−3","+2","+4","+5"],"answer":3,"why":"Vanadium has oxidation number +5. Let V be x. For the neutral compound, 10(+2) + 6x + 6(4)(−2) + 2(−1) = 0. Therefore 6x = 30 and x = +5.","tip":"Count every atom outside and inside brackets before solving.","strand":"Oxidation numbers"},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/12","sourceIndex":2,"stem":"In which of the following chemical species does V (vanadium) have the lowest oxidation number?\n(Given: The oxidation number of oxygen in each species is −2.)","context":"","options":["V₂O₃","VO₂","VO₂⁺","VO₄³−"],"answer":0,"why":"V is lowest in V₂O₃, where it is +3. The oxidation numbers of V are +3, +4, +5 and +5 respectively. The lowest value is therefore found in V₂O₃.","tip":"","strand":"Oxidation numbers"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/11","sourceIndex":3,"stem":"In which of the following compounds does nitrogen have the highest oxidation number?","context":"","options":["NF₃","N₂H₄","NaNH₂","HONH₂"],"answer":0,"why":"Nitrogen is +3 in NF₃. The nitrogen oxidation numbers are +3 in NF₃, −2 in N₂H₄, −3 in NaNH₂ and −1 in HONH₂.","tip":"","strand":"Oxidation numbers"},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/6","sourceIndex":4,"stem":"The conversion of nitrogen gas to nitric acid involves the following steps:","context":"N₂ → Step 1 NH₃ → Step 2 NO → Step 3 NO₂ → Step 4 HNO₃","options":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4"],"answer":0,"why":"Nitrogen is reduced in Step 1. In Step 1 nitrogen gains hydrogen and its oxidation number decreases from 0 in N₂ to −3 in NH₃.","tip":"Reduction can be identified by gain of hydrogen or a decrease in oxidation number.","strand":"Oxidation and reduction"},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/14","sourceIndex":5,"stem":"Consider the following reaction:","context":"(NH₄)₂Cr₂O₇(s) → Cr₂O₃(s) + N₂(g) + 4H₂O(g)\n(1) The oxidation number of chromium decreases.\n(2) Only covalent bonds are broken and formed.\n(3) Green solid turns to orange solid.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Only statement (1) is correct. Chromium decreases from +6 in dichromate to +3 in Cr₂O₃. Ionic as well as covalent interactions are involved, and orange ammonium dichromate forms green chromium(III) oxide—not the reverse.","tip":"Track both oxidation numbers and stated colour direction.","strand":"Redox changes"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/16","sourceIndex":6,"stem":"Consider the following reaction:","context":"Cl₂ + 2Br− → Br₂ + 2Cl−","options":["It loses electrons and is reduced.","It gains electrons and is reduced.","It loses electrons and is oxidised.","It gains electrons and is oxidised."],"answer":1,"why":"The oxidising agent gains electrons and is reduced. Cl₂ accepts electrons to form Cl−. An oxidising agent always causes oxidation of another species and is itself reduced.","tip":"","strand":"Oxidising agents"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/20","sourceIndex":8,"stem":"Pb is an element in Group IV of the Periodic Table and can form Pb²⁺ ion. Which statements are correct?","context":"(1) The change from Pb²⁺ ion to Pb atom is a reduction.\n(2) Both Pb atom and Pb²⁺ ion have the same number of protons.\n(3) Both Pb atom and Pb²⁺ ion have the same number of occupied electron shells.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"All three statements are correct. Pb²⁺ gains electrons to form Pb, so it is reduced. Ion formation does not change the nucleus. Removing two outer electrons does not empty lead’s sixth occupied shell.","tip":"","strand":"Electron transfer"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/14","sourceIndex":9,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a redox reaction?","context":"","options":["2AgBr(s) → 2Ag(s) + Br₂(g)","SO₂(g) + 2H₂S(g) → 3S(s) + 2H₂O(l)","2KClO₃(s) → 2KCl(s) + 3O₂(g)","Ca(HCO₃)₂(aq) → CaCO₃(s) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)"],"answer":3,"why":"The calcium hydrogencarbon decomposition is not redox. In option D no element changes oxidation number. Options A–C form elements from compounds and involve oxidation-number changes.","tip":"","strand":"Identifying redox"},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/12","sourceIndex":10,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a redox reaction?","context":"","options":["2Mg + SO₂ → 2MgO + S","CaCO₃ + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃ + CO₂","Cu₂O + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + Cu + H₂O","3CuS + 8HNO₃ → 3CuSO₄ + 8NO + 4H₂O"],"answer":1,"why":"Option B is not a redox reaction. No oxidation number changes in CaCO₃ + SiO₂. In A and C elements are formed from compounds, while in D nitrogen changes from +5 to +2.","tip":"","strand":"Identifying redox"},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/13","sourceIndex":11,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a redox reaction?","context":"","options":["BaCO₃(s) → BaO(s) + CO₂(g)","4KClO₃(s) → 3KClO₄(s) + KCl(s)","Cl₂(g) + H₂O(l) → HCl(aq) + HOCl(aq)","Zn(s) + CuSO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Cu(s)"],"answer":0,"why":"Barium carbonate decomposition is not redox. All oxidation numbers remain unchanged in option A. B and C involve disproportionation, and D is a metal-displacement redox reaction.","tip":"","strand":"Identifying redox"},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/21","sourceIndex":12,"stem":"Which of the following reactions involve oxidation?","context":"(1) CH₃CH=CHCH₃ + H₂ → CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃\n(2) 3Br₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaBr + NaBrO₃ + 3H₂O\n(3) 2Al + 2NaOH + 6H₂O → 2NaAl(OH)₄ + 3H₂","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"All three reactions involve oxidation. Hydrogen is oxidised from 0 to +1 in (1), bromine is partly oxidised to bromate in (2), and aluminium is oxidised from 0 to +3 in (3).","tip":"","strand":"Oxidation"},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/3","sourceIndex":13,"stem":"Which of the following processes does NOT involve oxidation and reduction?","context":"","options":["red wine turning sour","removing rust using white vinegar","combusting natural gas in a power station","removing nitrogen oxides in the catalytic converter of a car"],"answer":1,"why":"Removing rust with vinegar is not a redox process. Vinegar dissolves iron oxides by an acid reaction without changing oxidation states. The other processes oxidise ethanol or methane, or reduce nitrogen oxides.","tip":"","strand":"Everyday redox"},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/30","sourceIndex":14,"stem":"Which of the following ions can act as both an oxidising agent and a reducing agent?","context":"","options":["Fe²⁺(aq)","Cu²⁺(aq)","Cr₂O₇²−(aq)","MnO₄−(aq)"],"answer":0,"why":"Fe²⁺ can be oxidised or reduced. Fe²⁺ can lose an electron to form Fe³⁺ or gain electrons to form Fe(s). It can therefore act as either agent.","tip":"","strand":"Redox agents"},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/5","sourceIndex":15,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning francium (atomic number = 87) is correct?","context":"","options":["Francium has a higher melting point than potassium.","Francium forms cations more readily than potassium.","Francium is a weaker oxidising agent than potassium.","Francium has fewer occupied electron shells than potassium."],"answer":1,"why":"Francium forms cations more readily than potassium. Down Group I, outer electrons are farther from the nucleus and are lost more readily. Francium is therefore a stronger reducing agent and forms Fr⁺ more readily.","tip":"","strand":"Reducing power"},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/17","sourceIndex":16,"stem":"An aqueous solution of potassium iodide turns yellow with time due to the reaction:","context":"4KI(aq) + 2CO₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2K₂CO₃(aq) + 2I₂(aq)\n(1) KI(aq) is oxidised by O₂(g).\n(2) KI(aq) is oxidised by CO₂(g).\n(3) The yellow colour is due to the K₂CO₃(aq) formed.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Only statement (1) is correct. Iodide is oxidised to iodine by oxygen. Carbon dioxide supplies carbonate but is not the oxidising agent. The yellow colour is due to dissolved iodine, not potassium carbonate.","tip":"","strand":"Oxidising agents"},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/22","sourceIndex":17,"stem":"Which of the following processes involve redox reaction?","context":"(1) mixing methanol and ethanol\n(2) mixing chlorine and methane under sunlight\n(3) mixing ethene and acidified KMnO₄(aq)","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":2,"why":"Processes (2) and (3) are redox. Methanol and ethanol merely mix. Chlorination of methane and oxidation of ethene by acidified permanganate both involve changes in oxidation number.","tip":"","strand":"Redox reactions"},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/10","sourceIndex":18,"stem":"Which of the following reagents does NOT react with copper?","context":"","options":["2 M H₂SO₄","2 M HNO₃","16 M H₂SO₄","16 M HNO₃"],"answer":0,"why":"Dilute sulphuric acid does not react with copper. Copper is below hydrogen in the electrochemical series. Dilute sulphuric acid is not sufficiently oxidising, while dilute/concentrated nitric acid and hot concentrated sulphuric acid oxidise copper.","tip":"","strand":"Oxidising acids"},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/4","sourceIndex":19,"stem":"Which of the following processes would give a colourless gas and a colourless solution?","context":"","options":["adding lead granules to hot water","adding zinc foils to concentrated nitric acid","adding copper powder to concentrated sulphuric acid","adding magnesium ribbons to dilute hydrochloric acid"],"answer":3,"why":"Magnesium with dilute hydrochloric acid gives colourless H₂ and MgCl₂ solution. Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂. Both hydrogen and magnesium chloride solution are colourless.","tip":"","strand":"Metal reactions"},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/24","sourceIndex":20,"stem":"Consider the following statements and choose the best answer:","context":"1st statement Copper can react with dilute nitric acid but not with dilute ethanoic acid.\n2nd statement Nitric acid is a strong acid while ethanoic acid is a weak acid.","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement correctly explains the 1st.","Both statements are true but the 2nd statement does NOT correctly explain the 1st.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":1,"why":"Both statements are true, but the explanation is not causal. Copper reacts with dilute nitric acid because nitric acid is an oxidising agent, not simply because it is a strong acid. Ethanoic acid is weak and non-oxidising under these conditions.","tip":"","strand":"Oxidising acids"},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/22","sourceIndex":21,"stem":"Which reagents can distinguish between sodium sulphite and sodium sulphate?","context":"(1) iron(II) chloride solution\n(2) acidified potassium permanganate solution\n(3) concentrated nitric acid","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Acidified permanganate and concentrated nitric acid distinguish sulphite from sulphate. Sulphite is a reducing agent while sulphate is not. Both oxidising reagents react with sulphite; iron(II) chloride does not provide a distinguishing observation.","tip":"","strand":"Tests for reducing agents"},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/11","sourceIndex":23,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning zinc is correct?","context":"","options":["It forms a soluble oxide when placed in NH₃(aq).","It acts as a reducing agent when placed in HCl(aq).","It undergoes oxidation when placed in MgCl₂(aq).","It forms an acidic solution when placed in hot H₂O(l)."],"answer":1,"why":"Zinc acts as a reducing agent in hydrochloric acid. Zinc loses electrons to form Zn²⁺ while H⁺ gains electrons to form hydrogen. Zinc is therefore the reducing agent.","tip":"","strand":"Metals as reducing agents"},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/23","sourceIndex":24,"stem":"What would be observed when a few drops of concentrated nitric acid are added to KI(aq)?","context":"(1) A brown solution is formed.\n(2) A brown precipitate is formed.\n(3) A reddish brown gas is released.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":1,"why":"A brown iodine solution and reddish-brown nitrogen dioxide are formed. Concentrated nitric acid oxidises I− to I₂(aq) and is reduced to NO₂(g): 2NO₃− + 4H⁺ + 2I− → 2NO₂ + 2H₂O + I₂.","tip":"","strand":"Oxidising acids"},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/23","sourceIndex":26,"stem":"Two gas jars contain CO₂(g) and SO₂(g). Which tests can distinguish the gases?","context":"(1) adding bromine water\n(2) adding sodium hydroxide solution\n(3) adding concentrated nitric acid","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":1,"why":"Bromine water and concentrated nitric acid distinguish SO₂ from CO₂. SO₂ is reducing: it decolourises bromine water and reduces concentrated nitric acid to brown NO₂. Both gases are acidic and show no useful visible difference with NaOH.","tip":"","strand":"Gas tests"},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/12","sourceIndex":29,"stem":"Refer to the half-equations:","context":"C₂O₄²−(aq) → 2CO₂(g) + 2e−\nMnO₄−(aq) + 8H⁺(aq) + 5e− → Mn²⁺(aq) + 4H₂O(l)","options":["6.00 cm³","12.00 cm³","15.00 cm³","75.00 cm³"],"answer":1,"why":"12.00 cm³ of permanganate is required. The ratio C₂O₄²−:MnO₄− is 5:2. Oxalate moles = 0.020 × 15.00/1000 = 3.00×10−⁴. Permanganate moles = 1.20×10−⁴, so volume at 0.010 M is 0.0120 dm³ = 12.00 cm³.","tip":"","strand":"Redox stoichiometry"},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/15","sourceIndex":30,"stem":"P, Q and R are metals. Only Q and R give a colourless gas with dilute HCl. When zinc is added to their chloride solutions, only R chloride shows no observable change. What is the increasing order of reducing power?","context":"","options":["R < Q < P","Q < P < R","P < Q < R","P < R < Q"],"answer":2,"why":"The increasing order is P < Q < R. P is least reactive because it does not displace hydrogen. Zinc displaces Q but not R, so R is more reactive than zinc and Q is less reactive than zinc. Therefore P < Q < R.","tip":"","strand":"Reducing power"},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/13","sourceIndex":31,"stem":"The tendency of six ionic species to be reduced increases in the order:","context":"Ba²⁺(aq) < Na⁺(aq) < Mg²⁺(aq) < H⁺(aq) < Cu²⁺(aq) < Hg²⁺(aq)","options":["Ba(s) does NOT react with H⁺(aq).","Na(s) has a stronger reducing power than Hg(l).","Hg²⁺(aq) is the weakest oxidising agent among the six species.","A displacement reaction occurs when Cu(s) is immersed in MgSO₄(aq)."],"answer":1,"why":"Sodium has stronger reducing power than mercury. Reversing the reduction trend gives reducing power of metals: Ba > Na > Mg > H₂ > Cu > Hg. Hence Na is a stronger reducing agent than Hg.","tip":"","strand":"Electrochemical series"},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/16","sourceIndex":32,"stem":"Which chemicals can distinguish concentrated hydrochloric acid from concentrated nitric acid?","context":"(1) sodium carbonate solid\n(2) silver nitrate solution\n(3) copper metal","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Silver nitrate and copper distinguish the two acids. AgNO₃ forms AgCl with hydrochloric acid only. Copper reacts with oxidising concentrated nitric acid to give brown NO₂, but not with concentrated hydrochloric acid. Both acids release CO₂ from carbonate.","tip":"","strand":"Qualitative redox tests"},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/21","sourceIndex":33,"stem":"Which of the following is/are secondary cell(s)?","context":"(1) alkaline manganese cell\n(2) lithium ion cell\n(3) nickel metal hydride cell","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Lithium-ion and nickel-metal hydride cells are secondary cells. Secondary cells are rechargeable. Lithium-ion and nickel-metal hydride cells are rechargeable, whereas an alkaline manganese cell is normally treated as a primary cell.","tip":"","strand":"Types of chemical cells"},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/8","sourceIndex":40,"stem":"In a hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell, hydrogen is","context":"","options":["oxidised at the anode.","oxidised at the cathode.","reduced at the anode.","reduced at the cathode."],"answer":0,"why":"Hydrogen is oxidised at the anode. Hydrogen supplies electrons during discharge; oxidation always occurs at the anode.","tip":"","strand":"Fuel cells"},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/8","sourceIndex":42,"stem":"Which statement concerning a hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["It contains a catalyst.","Water is formed during discharge.","Oxygen gas is passed to the anode.","Hydrogen gas acts as the reducing agent."],"answer":2,"why":"Oxygen should be supplied to the cathode, not the anode. Oxygen is reduced at the cathode. Hydrogen is oxidised at the anode and acts as the reducing agent; water is the overall product.","tip":"","strand":"Fuel cells"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/22","sourceIndex":43,"stem":"Which statements concerning hydrogen–oxygen fuel cells are correct?","context":"(1) When used to power vehicles, they are more environmentally friendly than petrol engines.\n(2) When used in space stations, they can produce drinking water in addition to energy.\n(3) As back-up power in hospitals, they do not produce noise pollution.","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"All three statements are correct. Fuel cells produce water at the point of use, can supply useful water in spacecraft and operate quietly because ion/electron movement has no noisy combustion or moving engine parts.","tip":"","strand":"Fuel-cell applications"},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/18","sourceIndex":44,"stem":"Which statements concerning a hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell are correct?","context":"(1) It produces a non-polluting product.\n(2) Its membrane selectively allows hydroxide ions to pass through.\n(3) It can continuously produce electricity while hydrogen and oxygen are supplied under operating conditions.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":2,"why":"Statements (1) and (3) are correct. The overall product is water, and a fuel cell continues while reactants are supplied. In the cell model used here there is no hydroxide-selective membrane.","tip":"","strand":"Fuel cells"},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/2","sourceIndex":45,"stem":"Which of the following processes would NOT give oxygen?","context":"","options":["heating mercury(II) oxide strongly","electrolysis of dilute sulphuric acid","fractional distillation of liquefied air","passing steam over heated magnesium"],"answer":3,"why":"Steam over hot magnesium gives hydrogen, not oxygen. Magnesium reduces steam to hydrogen while forming magnesium oxide. The other processes can produce or separate oxygen.","tip":"","strand":"Electrolysis products"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/3","sourceIndex":46,"stem":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["Cracking of heavy oil can give ethene.","Electrolysis of sea water can give chlorine.","Strong heating of limestone can give oxygen.","Fractional distillation of liquefied air can give nitrogen."],"answer":2,"why":"Heating limestone gives carbon dioxide, not oxygen. CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g). The other statements describe valid industrial/laboratory processes.","tip":"","strand":"Electrolysis products"},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/19","sourceIndex":53,"stem":"Which processes can form a halogen?","context":"(1) Electrolyse concentrated KCl(aq).\n(2) Add Na₂SO₄(s) to concentrated HBr(aq).\n(3) Add KI(s) to acidified KMnO₄(aq).","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":2,"why":"Processes (1) and (3) form halogens. Concentrated chloride is discharged to Cl₂ at the anode. Acidified permanganate oxidises I− to I₂. Sodium sulphate does not oxidise HBr.","tip":"","strand":"Halogen formation"},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/11","sourceIndex":60,"stem":"Sodium sulphite is added slowly to acidified potassium permanganate until reaction is complete. How does [H⁺] change?","context":"SO₃²− + H₂O → SO₄²− + 2H⁺ + 2e−\nMnO₄− + 8H⁺ + 5e− → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O","options":["decrease","decrease then increase","increase","increase then decrease"],"answer":0,"why":"The hydrogen-ion concentration decreases. Multiply the sulphite half-equation by 5 and the permanganate half-equation by 2. Ten H⁺ are produced but sixteen are consumed, giving net consumption of six H⁺.","tip":"","strand":"Balancing redox half-equations"}]},{"topic":8,"name":"Chemical Reactions and Energy","href":"chemical-reactions-energy.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/20/1A/13","sourceIndex":0,"stem":"The enthalpy changes for the conversions W → X, X → Y and Y → Z are −150, +100 and +60 kJ mol⁻¹ respectively. Which combination is correct?","context":"W −150 → X +100 → Y +60 → Z","options":["W → Z: exothermic; Z → X: endothermic","W → Z: exothermic; Z → X: exothermic","W → Z: endothermic; Z → X: exothermic","W → Z: endothermic; Z → X: endothermic"],"answer":2,"why":"Add enthalpy changes along a route and reverse the sign when a route is reversed. ΔH(W → Z) = −150 + 100 + 60 = +10 kJ mol⁻¹, so W → Z is endothermic. ΔH(X → Z) = +160 kJ mol⁻¹, so ΔH(Z → X) = −160 kJ mol⁻¹ and is exothermic.","tip":"Sketch the states in order, total each requested route, then use ΔH(reverse) = −ΔH(forward).","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/22","sourceIndex":1,"stem":"Which processes are exothermic?\n(1) Placing calcium oxide in water\n(2) Placing a zinc strip in copper(II) sulphate solution\n(3) Passing hydrogen chloride gas into sodium hydroxide solution","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"Hydration, displacement and acid-alkali neutralisation all release heat. CaO hydration is strongly exothermic. Zinc displaces copper from Cu²⁺ solution and releases heat. HCl(g) dissolves and neutralises OH⁻; both processes release heat. Therefore all three are exothermic.","tip":"Classify the chemical change, not just the physical appearance of the experiment.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/22","sourceIndex":2,"stem":"Which processes are exothermic?\n(1) Thermal decomposition of mercury(II) oxide solid\n(2) Dilution of concentrated sulphuric acid with water\n(3) Reaction of magnesium ribbon with dilute hydrochloric acid","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":2,"why":"Thermal decomposition absorbs heat; acid dilution and metal-acid reaction release heat. Statement (1) is endothermic because heat is required for decomposition. Diluting concentrated H₂SO₄ and reacting Mg with HCl both release heat, so (2) and (3) are correct.","tip":"The word ‘thermal decomposition’ is a strong clue that energy is absorbed.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/8","sourceIndex":3,"stem":"Which process is endothermic?","context":"","options":["Dilution of concentrated sulphuric acid with water","Thermal decomposition of limestone","Reaction of quicklime with water","Combustion of ethanol"],"answer":1,"why":"Breaking down calcium carbonate requires a continuous heat supply. CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g) is a thermal decomposition and absorbs heat. The other three processes release heat.","tip":"Separate decomposition from combustion: decomposition usually needs heating; combustion releases heat.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/22","sourceIndex":4,"stem":"Which processes are endothermic?\n(1) Melting of wax\n(2) Cracking of heavy oil\n(3) Adding zinc powder to CuSO₄(aq)","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":0,"why":"Melting and cracking absorb energy; metal displacement releases heat. Melting requires latent heat, and cracking needs energy to break C−C bonds. Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu is an exothermic displacement reaction.","tip":"Remember that a physical change can still be endothermic.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/18","sourceIndex":5,"stem":"Which combinations of reaction and sign of enthalpy change are correct?\n(1) 2H₂O(l) → 2H₂(g) + O₂(g): positive\n(2) 2CO(g) + O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g): positive\n(3) 2Na(s) + 2H₂O(l) → 2NaOH(aq) + H₂(g): negative","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":2,"why":"Decomposition of water is endothermic; combustion and sodium-water reaction are exothermic. Reaction (1) is the reverse of hydrogen combustion, so ΔH is positive. CO combustion in (2) releases heat, so its ΔH is negative. Sodium reacting with water in (3) is exothermic, so ΔH is negative.","tip":"Write ‘combustion = negative ΔH’ before checking statement signs.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/15","sourceIndex":6,"stem":"Which statement concerning the combustion of ethanol is correct?","context":"","options":["The combustion of ethanol is endothermic.","The total enthalpy of products is less than that of reactants.","Ethanol burns immediately when exposed to air at room conditions.","Bond breaking requires more energy than is released by bond formation."],"answer":1,"why":"For an exothermic reaction, products lie at lower enthalpy than reactants. Ethanol combustion has ΔH < 0. More energy is released when product bonds form than is absorbed when reactant bonds break, so the products have lower total enthalpy.","tip":"For combustion: ΔH < 0, H(products) < H(reactants), and an ignition source is still needed.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/12","sourceIndex":7,"stem":"Which reaction represents an enthalpy change of neutralisation?\n(1) CO₂(g) + CaO(s) → CaCO₃(s), ΔH₁\n(2) NH₃(g) + HBr(g) → NH₄Br(s), ΔH₂\n(3) HF(aq) + NaOH(aq) → NaF(aq) + H₂O(l), ΔH₃\n(4) NaHCO₃(aq) + HCl(aq) → NaCl(aq) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(l), ΔH₄","context":"","options":["ΔH₁","ΔH₂","ΔH₃","ΔH₄"],"answer":2,"why":"Neutralisation forms one mole of water from an acid and an alkali in aqueous solution. HF(aq) supplies acid and NaOH(aq) supplies OH⁻. Their reaction forms water and an aqueous salt, so ΔH₃ is an enthalpy change of neutralisation.","tip":"Look for acid(aq) + alkali(aq) → salt(aq) + H₂O(l).","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/21","sourceIndex":8,"stem":"Which statements are correct?\n(1) The standard enthalpy change of formation of graphite is zero.\n(2) The standard enthalpy change of combustion of carbon monoxide is negative.\n(3) The standard enthalpy change of formation of carbon monoxide equals the standard enthalpy change of combustion of graphite.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":0,"why":"Elements in their standard states have ΔH°f = 0; combustion is exothermic. Graphite is carbon’s standard state, so (1) is true. CO combustion releases heat, so (2) is true. Formation of CO and complete combustion of graphite have different products and are not the same enthalpy change, so (3) is false.","tip":"Compare complete thermochemical equations before declaring two enthalpy changes equal.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/24","sourceIndex":9,"stem":"1st statement: Ethene and but-1-ene have the same standard enthalpy change of combustion.\n2nd statement: Ethene and but-1-ene have the same empirical formula.","context":"","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd explains the 1st.","Both statements are true but the 2nd does not explain the 1st.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":2,"why":"Same empirical formula does not mean the same molar enthalpy of combustion. Both alkenes have empirical formula CH₂, so the second statement is true. One mole of but-1-ene contains twice as many carbon and hydrogen atoms as one mole of ethene, so their molar enthalpies of combustion are not the same.","tip":"Enthalpy change is quoted per mole of the substance as written, not per empirical-formula unit.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/24","sourceIndex":12,"stem":"1st statement: The standard enthalpy change of formation of a compound must be negative.\n2nd statement: Under standard conditions, a compound must be energetically more stable than its constituent elements.","context":"","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd explains the 1st.","Both statements are true but the 2nd does not explain the 1st.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":3,"why":"A compound may have a positive or negative standard enthalpy of formation. Some compounds are formed endothermically from their elements and have positive ΔH°f. Such compounds can be at higher enthalpy than their constituent elements. Therefore neither statement must be true.","tip":"Be cautious with ‘must’: ΔH°f is defined by a route, not by a universal stability rule.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/14","sourceIndex":13,"stem":"Which pair of reactants would react in water to release the largest amount of heat?","context":"","options":["1 mol HCl and 1 mol KOH","1 mol H₂SO₄ and 2 mol KOH","1 mol (COOH)₂ and 2 mol KOH","1 mol CH₃COOH and 1 mol KOH"],"answer":1,"why":"Strong acid-strong alkali neutralisation releases about the same heat per mole of water formed. H₂SO₄ and 2 mol KOH can form 2 mol H₂O using a strong acid and strong alkali, so approximately two neutralisation equivalents of heat are released. The weak acids in C and D require energy for ionisation.","tip":"Count moles of water formed, then check whether the acid and alkali are strong.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/12","sourceIndex":14,"stem":"Mixing 40 cm³ of 0.5 M HCl with 40 cm³ of 0.5 M NaOH gives a temperature rise ΔT. What is the rise when 60 cm³ of 1.0 M HCl is mixed with 60 cm³ of 0.5 M NaOH?","context":"","options":["0.75ΔT","ΔT","1.5ΔT","2ΔT"],"answer":1,"why":"Temperature rise depends on heat released per total heat capacity of the mixture. First mixture: 0.020 mol water forms in 80 cm³. Second: OH⁻ is limiting, so 0.030 mol water forms in 120 cm³. Both ratios are 0.00025 mol cm⁻³, so heat released per unit mass is unchanged and the temperature rise is ΔT.","tip":"For equal-density solutions, compare moles neutralised ÷ total volume.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/15","sourceIndex":17,"stem":"For which reaction must its enthalpy change be determined by indirect methods?","context":"","options":["Zn(s) + CuSO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Cu(s)","2C(s) + O₂(g) → 2CO(g)","CH₃CH₂OH(l) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l)","MgO(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l)"],"answer":1,"why":"A direct measurement is unsuitable when the desired product cannot be formed cleanly as the sole product. Carbon normally burns to a mixture or to CO₂; it is not practical to make only CO quantitatively under standard calorimetry conditions. Hess’s law is therefore required for reaction B.","tip":"Ask whether the written reaction can be carried out completely and selectively in one experiment.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/21","sourceIndex":18,"stem":"Which statements are correct?\n(1) ΔH°f of NH₃(g) can be determined directly from experiment.\n(2) ΔH°c of H₂NNH₂(l) is negative.\n(3) ΔH°f of N₂(g) is zero.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":2,"why":"Combustion is exothermic and an element in its standard state has zero ΔH°f. Hydrazine combustion releases heat, so (2) is true. N₂(g) is nitrogen’s standard state, so (3) is true. Direct formation of pure NH₃ from its elements is unsuitable for direct calorimetry because the reaction is reversible and does not go to completion.","tip":"‘Can be determined directly’ requires a clean, complete, measurable reaction.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/20","sourceIndex":19,"stem":"Which statements are correct?\n(1) ΔH°f of liquid nitrogen is not zero.\n(2) ΔH°f of a compound must be negative.\n(3) ΔH°f of CO(g) can be determined directly.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Only an element in its standard state has zero standard enthalpy of formation. At standard conditions nitrogen’s standard state is N₂(g), not liquid nitrogen, so (1) is true. Compounds can have positive ΔH°f, and CO cannot be formed cleanly as the sole combustion product of carbon, so (2) and (3) are false.","tip":"Check both identity and physical state when applying ΔH°f(element) = 0.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/12","sourceIndex":20,"stem":"Given:\n2Mg(s) + O₂(g) → 2MgO(s), ΔH₁\nMg(s) + ½O₂(g) + CO₂(g) → MgCO₃(s), ΔH₂\nMgCO₃(s) → MgO(s) + CO₂(g), ΔH₃\nWhat is ΔH₃?","context":"","options":["ΔH₁ − ΔH₂","ΔH₂ − ΔH₁","ΔH₂ − ½ΔH₁","½ΔH₁ − ΔH₂"],"answer":3,"why":"Scale equations before adding them in a Hess cycle. Half of reaction 1 gives Mg + ½O₂ → MgO with ½ΔH₁. Reverse reaction 2 to give MgCO₃ → Mg + ½O₂ + CO₂ with −ΔH₂. Adding gives the target, so ΔH₃ = ½ΔH₁ − ΔH₂.","tip":"Write the target first; then reverse and scale source equations to cancel species.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/23","sourceIndex":21,"stem":"For 2Na(s) + O₂(g) → Na₂O₂(s), ΔH° = −515 kJ mol⁻¹, which statements are correct?\n(1) ΔH°f[Na₂O₂(s)] = −515 kJ mol⁻¹.\n(2) ΔH°c[Na(s)] = −515 kJ mol⁻¹.\n(3) Formation of 1 mol Na₂O₂ under standard conditions releases 515 kJ.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":1,"why":"A molar enthalpy applies to the reaction as written and to the defined amount of substance. The equation forms 1 mol Na₂O₂ from elements in standard states, so (1) is true. It releases 515 kJ for the equation as written, so (3) is true. Statement (2) incorrectly assigns −515 kJ to combustion of 1 mol Na.","tip":"Check the stoichiometric amount attached to every ‘per mole’ definition.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/18","sourceIndex":22,"stem":"Given 2H₂O(l) → 2H₂(g) + O₂(g), ΔH° = +x kJ mol⁻¹, which statements are correct?\n(1) ΔH°f[H₂O(l)] = −0.5x kJ mol⁻¹.\n(2) ΔH°f[H₂O(l)] = +0.5x kJ mol⁻¹.\n(3) ΔH°c[H₂(g)] = −x kJ mol⁻¹.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Reverse the equation and divide ΔH by the stoichiometric factor. Reversing gives 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O with ΔH = −x. Formation or combustion of 1 mol H₂O/H₂ is therefore −x/2. Statement (1) is true; statement (3) incorrectly uses −x for one mole.","tip":"When an equation is divided by n, divide its ΔH by n too.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/14","sourceIndex":23,"stem":"Given ΔHf values H₂O(l) = −286, Na₂O(s) = −414 and NaOH(s) = −425 kJ mol⁻¹, find ΔH for Na₂O(s) + H₂O(l) → 2NaOH(s).","context":"","options":["+275 kJ mol⁻¹","−150 kJ mol⁻¹","−722 kJ mol⁻¹","−1125 kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":1,"why":"Use ΣΔHf(products) − ΣΔHf(reactants), including coefficients. ΔH = 2(−425) − [(−414) + (−286)] = −850 + 700 = −150 kJ mol⁻¹.","tip":"Write P − R above the calculation before substituting numbers.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/7","sourceIndex":24,"stem":"At 298 K: ΔH°c[H₂(g)] = −286, ΔH°c[C(graphite)] = −394 and ΔH°c[CH₃CH₂OH(l)] = −1371 kJ mol⁻¹. What is ΔH°f of ethanol?","context":"","options":["−275 kJ mol⁻¹","+275 kJ mol⁻¹","+691 kJ mol⁻¹","−3017 kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":0,"why":"Use a combustion Hess cycle: formation plus fuel combustion equals combustion of the elements. Combusting 2C and 3H₂ gives 2(−394) + 3(−286) = −1646 kJ mol⁻¹. Hence ΔH°f(ethanol) + (−1371) = −1646, so ΔH°f = −275 kJ mol⁻¹.","tip":"In a combustion cycle, all routes finish at the same CO₂ and H₂O products.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/9","sourceIndex":25,"stem":"Given:\nB₂H₆(g) + 3O₂(g) → B₂O₃(s) + 3H₂O(l), −2170 kJ mol⁻¹\nB(s) + ¾O₂(g) → ½B₂O₃(s), −635 kJ mol⁻¹\nH₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → H₂O(l), −286 kJ mol⁻¹\nFind ΔHf of B₂H₆(g).","context":"","options":["+42 kJ mol⁻¹","+614 kJ mol⁻¹","+677 kJ mol⁻¹","+1249 kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":0,"why":"Scale formation data to match the products in the combustion equation. ΔHf(B₂O₃) = 2(−635) = −1270. Product formation total = −1270 + 3(−286) = −2128. Using −2170 = −2128 − ΔHf(B₂H₆) gives ΔHf(B₂H₆) = +42 kJ mol⁻¹.","tip":"Convert every half-mole formation equation to the amount used before applying Hess’s law.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/9","sourceIndex":26,"stem":"Given ΔH°f[H₂O(l)] = −286, ΔH°c[propane] = −2222 and ΔH°f[CO₂(g)] = −394 kJ mol⁻¹, find ΔH°f of propane.","context":"","options":["−52 kJ mol⁻¹","+52 kJ mol⁻¹","−104 kJ mol⁻¹","+104 kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":2,"why":"Balance propane combustion before substituting formation enthalpies. C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O. −2222 = [3(−394) + 4(−286)] − ΔH°f(propane) = −2326 − ΔH°f. Therefore ΔH°f = −104 kJ mol⁻¹.","tip":"Always balance C and H first, then O, before using enthalpy data.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/18","sourceIndex":27,"stem":"Complete combustion of 0.050 mol propane releases 111 kJ. Given ΔH°f[H₂O(l)] = −286 and ΔH°f[CO₂(g)] = −394 kJ mol⁻¹, find ΔH°f of propane.","context":"","options":["−106 kJ mol⁻¹","+106 kJ mol⁻¹","−569 kJ mol⁻¹","+569 kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":0,"why":"Convert the measured heat to a molar combustion enthalpy before using Hess’s law. ΔH°c(propane) = −111/0.050 = −2220 kJ mol⁻¹. The product formation total is 3(−394) + 4(−286) = −2326. Thus −2220 = −2326 − ΔH°f, giving ΔH°f = −106 kJ mol⁻¹.","tip":"Heat released is negative ΔH; state the sign immediately after converting to per mole.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/15","sourceIndex":29,"stem":"When 7.89 g carbon monoxide burns completely, 80 kJ is released. Under the same conditions ΔHf[CO₂(g)] = −394 kJ mol⁻¹. Find ΔHf[CO(g)]. (Ar: C = 12.0, O = 16.0)","context":"","options":["−678 kJ mol⁻¹","−474 kJ mol⁻¹","−314 kJ mol⁻¹","−110 kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":3,"why":"First obtain ΔHc(CO), then relate it to formation enthalpies. Moles CO = 7.89/28.0 = 0.2818 mol, so ΔHc(CO) ≈ −80/0.2818 = −284 kJ mol⁻¹. For CO + ½O₂ → CO₂: −284 = −394 − ΔHf(CO), hence ΔHf(CO) = −110 kJ mol⁻¹.","tip":"For combustion: ΔHc = ΔHf(CO₂) − ΔHf(fuel), since ΔHf(O₂) = 0.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/14","sourceIndex":30,"stem":"For 2FeO(s) + ½O₂(g) → Fe₂O₃(s), ΔHf[FeO(s)] = −270 and ΔHf[Fe₂O₃(s)] = −820 kJ mol⁻¹. Which expression gives the reaction enthalpy?","context":"","options":["[(−820 × ½) − (−270)] kJ mol⁻¹","[−270 − (−820 × ½)] kJ mol⁻¹","[−820 − (−270 × 2)] kJ mol⁻¹","[(−270 × 2) − (−820)] kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":2,"why":"Apply products minus reactants with the equation’s coefficients. ΔH = ΔHf(Fe₂O₃) − [2ΔHf(FeO) + ½ΔHf(O₂)] = −820 − [2(−270) + 0]. This is option C.","tip":"Coefficients multiply ΔHf values; elemental O₂ contributes zero.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/12","sourceIndex":31,"stem":"ΔH°neut for NaOH(aq) + HCl(aq) is −57.30 kJ mol⁻¹, while that for NH₃(aq) + HCl(aq) is −52.22 kJ mol⁻¹. Which statement best explains the difference?","context":"","options":["Strong ionic bonds exist between oppositely charged ions in NaOH(aq).","NH₃(aq) + H₂O(l) → NH₄⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) is endothermic.","Neutralising one mole HCl requires the same number of OH⁻ ions from NH₃ and NaOH.","Neutralising one mole HCl requires a smaller volume of NaOH than NH₃ of the same concentration."],"answer":1,"why":"Ionisation of a weak alkali absorbs part of the heat released by neutralisation. NaOH is fully dissociated, but NH₃ is a weak base. Energy is absorbed as NH₃ reacts with water to generate NH₄⁺ and OH⁻, so the net neutralisation enthalpy is less negative.","tip":"Weak acids or bases make ΔHneut less negative because ionisation is endothermic.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/23","sourceIndex":32,"stem":"Which reactions have a negative enthalpy change?\n(1) Ba(OH)₂(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → BaCl₂(aq) + 2H₂O(l)\n(2) CaO(s) + H₂O(l) → Ca(OH)₂(s)\n(3) C₁₂H₂₆(l) + 15O₂(g) → 6CO₂(g) + 5CO(g) + C(s) + 13H₂O(l)","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"Neutralisation, quicklime hydration and hydrocarbon combustion all release heat. Reaction (1) is strong acid-strong base neutralisation. Reaction (2) is the exothermic slaking of lime. Reaction (3) is incomplete combustion, but oxidation still releases heat. All three have ΔH < 0.","tip":"‘Incomplete’ combustion does not mean endothermic; it means less energy is released.","strand":""}]},{"topic":9,"name":"Rate of Reaction","href":"rate-of-reaction.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/24/1A/25","sourceIndex":3,"stem":"An experiment was performed to study the rate of the following reaction:\nX₂(g) → 2X(g)\nIn a closed container of fixed volume at a certain temperature, the concentration of X₂(g) decreased from 0.10 mol dm⁻³ to 0.04 mol dm⁻³ in the first 4 minutes.\nWhat is the average rate of formation of X(g) in the first 4 minutes?","context":"","options":["0.010 mol dm⁻³ min⁻¹","0.015 mol dm⁻³ min⁻¹","0.020 mol dm⁻³ min⁻¹","0.030 mol dm⁻³ min⁻¹"],"answer":3,"why":"[X₂] decreases by 0.06 mol dm−³ in 4 minutes, so its average rate of disappearance is 0.015 mol dm−³ min−¹. Since each mole of X₂ forms two moles of X, X forms at twice that rate.","tip":"","strand":"Rate curves & calculations"},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/33","sourceIndex":6,"stem":"For which reactions can their progress be followed by colorimetry?\n2MnO₄⁻(aq) + 5C₂O₄²⁻(aq) + 16H⁺(aq) → 2Mn²⁺(aq) + 10CO₂(g) + 8H₂O(l) SO₃²⁻(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) → SO₂(aq) + H₂O(l) Br₂(aq) + HCO₂H(aq) → 2Br⁻(aq) + CO₂(g) + 2H⁺(aq)","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":1,"why":"Reaction (1) consumes purple MnO₄−, and Reaction (3) consumes coloured Br₂(aq), so their colour intensity changes with progress. All principal species in Reaction (2) are colourless, so colorimetry is unsuitable.","tip":"","strand":"Monitoring reaction progress"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/34","sourceIndex":7,"stem":"Consider the following reaction:\nBr₂(aq) + HCOOH(aq) → CO₂(g) + 2HBr(aq)\nWhich quantities can be measured in order to follow the progress of the reaction?\nthe volume of gas formed the turbidity of the reaction mixture the colour intensity of the reaction mixture","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":1,"why":"CO₂(g) is formed, so gas volume increases. Br₂(aq) is coloured and is consumed, so colour intensity decreases. No suspended solid is formed, so the mixture does not become turbid.","tip":"","strand":"Monitoring reaction progress"},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/35","sourceIndex":8,"stem":"Consider the following reaction:\n5NaBr(aq) + NaBrO₃(aq) + 6HCl(aq) → 3Br₂(aq) + 6NaCl(aq) + 3H₂O(l)\nThe reactant solutions are colourless.\nWhich quantities can be measured in order to follow the progress of the reaction?\npH of the reaction mixture pressure of the reaction system colour intensity of the reaction mixture","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":1,"why":"HCl is consumed, so the pH rises as the reaction proceeds. Brown Br₂(aq) forms from colourless reactants, so colour intensity increases. No gas is produced, so the system pressure is not a suitable progress variable.","tip":"","strand":"Monitoring reaction progress"},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/34","sourceIndex":9,"stem":"Refer to the following reaction:\n2MnO₄⁻(aq) + 5C₂O₄²⁻(aq) + 16H⁺(aq) → 2Mn²⁺(aq) + 10CO₂(g) + 8H₂O(l)\nWhich quantities can be measured in order to follow the progress of the reaction?\nmass of H₂O(l) formed pressure of the reaction system colour intensity of the reaction system","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":2,"why":"CO₂(g) is formed, so pressure increases in a closed constant-volume system. Purple MnO₄− is consumed, so colour intensity decreases. The mass of water formed cannot be measured separately in the aqueous reaction mixture.","tip":"","strand":"Monitoring reaction progress"},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/25","sourceIndex":12,"stem":"Under room conditions, the initial rate of the reaction between excess zinc granules and 100 cm³ of 1.0 M HCl(aq) is measured as r . Using the same amount of zinc granules under room conditions, with which solution would the zinc granules react to give the same initial rate r ?","context":"","options":["50 cm³ of 2.0 M HCl(aq)","200 cm³ of 1.0 M HCl(aq)","100 cm³ of 1.0 M H₂SO₄(aq)","100 cm³ of 1.0 M CH₃COOH(aq)"],"answer":1,"why":"With the same zinc granules and temperature, 200 cm³ of 1.0 M HCl provides the same initial [H⁺] at the zinc surface as the original solution, so the initial rate is unchanged. The larger total amount of acid affects duration or final amount only if limiting.","tip":"","strand":"Factors affecting reaction rate"},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/28","sourceIndex":16,"stem":"Which pair of chemicals, upon mixing at the same temperature, has the highest rate of gas formation?","context":"","options":["0.10 g of Zn powder and 100 cm³ of 1.0 M HCl(aq)","0.10 g of Zn granules and 200 cm³ of 1.0 M HCl(aq)","0.10 g of Zn granules and 200 cm³ of 1.0 M H₂SO₄(aq)","0.10 g of Zn powder and 100 cm³ of 1.0 M H₂SO₄(aq)"],"answer":3,"why":"Zinc powder exposes more surface area than granules. At the same stated molarity, sulfuric acid supplies a higher concentration of H⁺ than hydrochloric acid for this comparison. Option D combines powder with sulfuric acid, so it gives the greatest frequency of effective collisions and the highest gas-formation rate.","tip":"","strand":"Factors affecting reaction rate"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/33","sourceIndex":19,"stem":"Which statements are correct?\nMagnesium oxide dissolves faster in 1 M HCl(aq) than in 1 M CH₃CO₂H(aq). Powdered marble dissolves faster in 1 M HCl(aq) than granular marble does. H₂O₂(aq) decomposes faster in the presence of MnO₂(s) than without MnO₂(s).","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"HCl is fully ionised and provides more H⁺ than 1 M ethanoic acid, so MgO dissolves faster. Powdered marble has a larger surface area than granules. MnO₂ provides an alternative pathway of lower activation energy for H₂O₂ decomposition. All three statements are correct.","tip":"","strand":"Factors affecting reaction rate"},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/34","sourceIndex":23,"stem":"Consider the following reaction:\n2H₂O₂(aq) MnO₂(s) → 2H₂O(l) + O₂(g)\nWhich statements are correct if the concentration of H₂O₂(aq) changes from 2 M to 1 M while the other conditions remain unchanged?\nThe consumption of MnO₂(s) will decrease. The rate of formation of O₂(g) will decrease. The volume of O₂(g) formed will decrease.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"MnO₂ is a catalyst, so it is regenerated and its net consumption does not depend on H₂O₂ concentration. Lowering the concentration lowers the initial rate. With the same solution volume, it also halves the moles of H₂O₂, so less O₂ is formed.","tip":"","strand":"Factors affecting reaction rate"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/28","sourceIndex":25,"stem":"Which chemical reaction is the fastest at room conditions?","context":"","options":["AgNO₃(aq) + NaCl(aq) → AgCl(s) + NaNO₃(aq)","Zn(s) + 2HCl(aq) → ZnCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)","4Fe(s) + 3O₂(g) → 2Fe₂O₃(s)","CH₄(g) + Br₂(in organic solvent) → CH₃Br(g) + HBr(g), in light"],"answer":0,"why":"Ag⁺(aq) and Cl−(aq) combine immediately when the two solutions mix, so AgCl precipitation is the fastest reaction listed. Zinc with acid is slower at a solid surface, rusting is slow, and methane bromination needs a photochemical chain process.","tip":"","strand":"Catalysis & relative reaction speed"},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/25","sourceIndex":26,"stem":"Consider the following reaction:\n4H₂(g) + Fe₃O₄(s) → 3Fe(s) + 4H₂O(l)\nWhat is the minimum volume of H₂(g) at room conditions required to form 0.168 g of Fe(s)?\nMolar volume of gas at room conditions = 24 dm³; relative atomic mass: Fe = 55.8","context":"","options":["24 cm³","48 cm³","96 cm³","192 cm³"],"answer":2,"why":"First find moles of Fe, then use 4 mol H₂ for every 3 mol Fe. Converting the required H₂ amount with 24 dm³ mol−¹ gives approximately 0.096 dm³, or 96 cm³.","tip":"","strand":"Integrated quantitative chemistry"},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/28","sourceIndex":27,"stem":"Consider the following reaction:\n2NaOH(aq) + N₂O₄(g) → NaNO₃(aq) + NaNO₂(aq) + H₂O(l)\nWhat is the minimum volume of 0.5 M NaOH(aq) needed to react completely with 480 cm³ of N₂O₄(g) at room conditions?\nMolar volume of gas at room conditions = 24 dm³","context":"","options":["8 cm³","12.5 cm³","40 cm³","80 cm³"],"answer":3,"why":"480 cm³ is 0.480 dm³, corresponding to 0.020 mol N₂O₄. The equation requires twice as many moles of NaOH, namely 0.040 mol. At 0.5 M, this requires 0.080 dm³ or 80 cm³.","tip":"","strand":"Integrated quantitative chemistry"},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/25","sourceIndex":28,"stem":"What is the theoretical volume of carbon dioxide obtainable at room temperature and pressure when 1.2 g of Na₂CO₃(s) reacts with 50 cm³ of 1.0 M HNO₃(aq)?\nMolar volume of gas at room temperature and pressure = 24 dm³; relative atomic masses: H = 1.0, C = 12.0, N = 14.0, O = 16.0, Na = 23.0","context":"","options":["272 cm³","544 cm³","600 cm³","1200 cm³"],"answer":0,"why":"Na₂CO₃ has molar mass 106 g mol−¹, so 1.2 g is 0.0113 mol. It needs 0.0226 mol HNO₃, while 0.050 mol is available; acid is in excess. The carbonate therefore forms 0.0113 mol CO₂, equal to about 272 cm³ at room conditions.","tip":"","strand":"Integrated quantitative chemistry"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/26","sourceIndex":29,"stem":"Consider the following reaction:\nC₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(l)\nIf 100 cm³ of C₃H₈(g) burns in 600 cm³ of O₂(g), what is the volume of the resulting gaseous mixture at room conditions?\nMolar volume of gas at room conditions = 24 dm³","context":"","options":["800 cm³","700 cm³","400 cm³","300 cm³"],"answer":2,"why":"100 cm³ of propane consumes 500 cm³ O₂, leaving 100 cm³ O₂. It forms 300 cm³ CO₂. Water is liquid at room conditions and is not counted in the gaseous mixture, so the total gas volume is 400 cm³.","tip":"","strand":"Integrated quantitative chemistry"},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/25","sourceIndex":30,"stem":"A mixture consists of methane and ethane. 50 cm³ of this mixture burns completely in oxygen to form 80 cm³ of carbon dioxide at room conditions. What is the volume of methane in the mixture at room conditions?\nMolar volume of gas at room conditions = 24 dm³","context":"","options":["10 cm³","20 cm³","30 cm³","40 cm³"],"answer":1,"why":"Let the methane volume be x cm³; ethane is then 50 − x. Methane forms an equal volume of CO₂, while ethane forms twice its volume. The total CO₂ equation gives x = 20 cm³.","tip":"","strand":"Integrated quantitative chemistry"}]},{"topic":10,"name":"Chemical Equilibrium","href":"chemical-equilibrium.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/14/1A/35","sourceIndex":3,"stem":"1st statement: At equilibrium, the forward reaction rate equals zero.\n2nd statement: At equilibrium, reactants do not react to give products.","context":"","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement correctly explains the 1st.","Both statements are true but the 2nd statement does not correctly explain the 1st.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":3,"why":"Equilibrium is dynamic. Forward and backward reactions continue at equal non-zero rates. Both statements are false.","tip":"Remember: equal rates, constant concentrations, ongoing reactions.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/36","sourceIndex":4,"stem":"1st statement: At equilibrium, reactant concentrations must equal product concentrations.\n2nd statement: At equilibrium, forward and backward rates are both zero.","context":"","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement correctly explains the 1st.","Both statements are true but the 2nd statement does not correctly explain the 1st.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":3,"why":"Equal rates do not mean equal concentrations or zero rates. Equilibrium concentrations are constant but need not be equal. Both rates remain equal and non-zero.","tip":"Separate ‘equal’ from ‘constant’.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/34","sourceIndex":5,"stem":"For 2CrO₄²⁻+2H⁺ ⇌ Cr₂O₇²⁻+H₂O, which statements are incorrect?\n(1) [CrO₄²⁻] must equal [Cr₂O₇²⁻].\n(2) Both reactions have stopped.\n(3) Moles CrO₄²⁻ must be double moles Cr₂O₇²⁻.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"Coefficients control changes, not equilibrium amounts. Equilibrium concentrations are not fixed by coefficients, and both reactions continue. All three are incorrect.","tip":"Coefficients give reaction ratios, not final mixture ratios.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/31","sourceIndex":6,"stem":"At equilibrium COCl₂(g) ⇌ CO(g)+Cl₂(g), which statements are correct?\n(1) [CO] must equal [Cl₂].\n(2) Rate of COCl₂ decomposition equals rate of CO formation.\n(3) Kc increases when volume increases.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"Equilibrium requires equal forward and backward rates. Statement (2) is correct. Initial products can make [CO] and [Cl₂] unequal, and volume does not change Kc at fixed temperature.","tip":"Only temperature changes Kc.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/29","sourceIndex":7,"stem":"HI ⇌ ½H₂+½I₂ has K₁; H₂+I₂ ⇌ 2HI has K₂. Which relationship is correct?","context":"","options":["K₁=1/K₂","K₁=1/K₂²","K₂=1/K₁","K₂=1/K₁²"],"answer":3,"why":"Reverse to invert K; multiply coefficients to raise K to that power. Doubling the first equation gives K₁², then reversing gives K₂=1/K₁².","tip":"Track reversal and scaling separately.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/26","sourceIndex":8,"stem":"CO+H₂O ⇌ CO₂+H₂ has Kc=0.8. What is Kc for the reverse reaction?","context":"","options":["0.8","1.25","0.8 mol⁻¹ dm³","1.25 mol⁻¹ dm³"],"answer":1,"why":"Reverse reactions have reciprocal equilibrium constants. K=1/0.8=1.25. Equal total concentration powers mean no unit.","tip":"Check both value and unit.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/26","sourceIndex":9,"stem":"K₁=1.3×10⁻³ for H₂A ⇌ H⁺+HA⁻ and K₂=3.1×10⁻⁶ for HA⁻ ⇌ H⁺+A²⁻. Find X for 2HA⁻ ⇌ H₂A+A²⁻.","context":"","options":["4.2×10²","2.4×10⁻³","4.0×10⁻⁹","2.5×10⁸"],"answer":1,"why":"Combine equations algebraically. Reverse the first step and add the second: X=K₂/K₁=2.4×10⁻³.","tip":"Write the target equation first.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/26","sourceIndex":10,"stem":"A+B ⇌ C+D has K₁; C+D ⇌ E+F+G has K₂; E+F+G ⇌ A+B has K₃. Which is correct?","context":"","options":["K₃=1/(K₁K₂); mol dm⁻³","K₃=1/(K₁K₂); mol⁻¹ dm³","K₃=K₁K₂; mol dm⁻³","K₃=K₁K₂; mol⁻¹ dm³"],"answer":1,"why":"Multiply successive equilibria, then invert for the return reaction. The first two give A+B ⇌ E+F+G with K₁K₂. Therefore K₃=1/(K₁K₂), with mol⁻¹ dm³.","tip":"Derive units from the final Kc expression.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/27","sourceIndex":11,"stem":"For X₂+3Y₂ ⇌ 2XY₃ in 2.0 dm³, equilibrium amounts are 0.4, 0.3 and 0.4 mol. Find Kc.","context":"","options":["3.3","6.7","14.8","59.3"],"answer":3,"why":"Convert amounts to concentrations. [X₂]=0.20, [Y₂]=0.15 and [XY₃]=0.20. Kc=0.20²/(0.20×0.15³)=59.3.","tip":"Box the Kc expression first.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/27","sourceIndex":12,"stem":"In 3.0 dm³, equilibrium amounts are 0.10 mol HI, 0.60 mol H₂ and 0.60 mol I₂. For 2HI ⇌ H₂+I₂, find Kc.","context":"","options":["0.4","3.6","9.0","36.0"],"answer":3,"why":"Use equilibrium concentrations and powers. Concentrations are 0.0333, 0.20 and 0.20. Kc=(0.20)(0.20)/(0.0333)²=36.0.","tip":"[HI] must be squared.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/29","sourceIndex":13,"stem":"For AgCl(s)+2NH₃ ⇌ [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺+Cl⁻, Kc=3.60×10⁻³ and equilibrium [NH₃]=8.93×10⁻² mol dm⁻³. Find [Cl⁻].","context":"","options":["2.87×10⁻⁵","3.21×10⁻⁴","5.36×10⁻³","1.79×10⁻² mol dm⁻³"],"answer":2,"why":"Omit pure AgCl; the products form equally. Let each product concentration be x. Kc=x²/(8.93×10⁻²)², so x=5.36×10⁻³.","tip":"Pure solids are omitted from Kc.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/31","sourceIndex":14,"stem":"For W+X ⇌ Y+Z, Kc=2.5. Initially 1.0 mol each of W and X are present with no products. How many moles of Y are at equilibrium?","context":"","options":["0.44","0.61","0.71","0.83"],"answer":1,"why":"Build an ICE table. If x mol products form, Kc=x²/(1−x)²=2.5. Thus x/(1−x)=√2.5 and x=0.61 mol.","tip":"Equal volumes cancel from this ratio.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/30","sourceIndex":15,"stem":"0.40 mol SO₂ and 0.60 mol O₂ react in 1.0 dm³: 2SO₂+O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃. At equilibrium 0.30 mol SO₃ is present. Find Kc.","context":"","options":["20","6.7","2.0","0.050 mol⁻¹ dm³"],"answer":0,"why":"Use stoichiometry to find remaining reactants. Forming 0.30 mol SO₃ consumes 0.30 SO₂ and 0.15 O₂. Kc=0.30²/(0.10²×0.45)=20 mol⁻¹ dm³.","tip":"Complete the ICE table first.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/30","sourceIndex":17,"stem":"CH₃COOH+C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅+H₂O. Initially 2.50 mol of each reactant; 0.85 mol acid remains. Find Kc.","context":"","options":["0.27","2.28","3.77","Cannot be determined without volume"],"answer":2,"why":"Equal coefficients make volume cancel. Extent=1.65 mol. Kc=(1.65²)/(0.85²)=3.77.","tip":"For 1:1 ⇌ 1:1, mole ratios may be used.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/27","sourceIndex":19,"stem":"For Fe³⁺+SCN⁻ ⇌ FeSCN²⁺, Fe(NO₃)₃ is added. At the new equilibrium, which pair is correct?","context":"","options":["Backward rate decreased; [FeSCN²⁺] unchanged","Backward rate decreased; [FeSCN²⁺] increased","Backward rate increased; [FeSCN²⁺] unchanged","Backward rate increased; [FeSCN²⁺] increased"],"answer":3,"why":"Added Fe³⁺ shifts equilibrium toward the complex. More FeSCN²⁺ forms. Its higher concentration makes the new backward rate higher; forward and backward rates are again equal.","tip":"Compare the two equilibrium states.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/31","sourceIndex":20,"stem":"For 2SO₂+O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, which statements are correct when volume decreases at constant temperature?\n(1) Kc increases.\n(2) Equilibrium shifts right.\n(3) SO₃ decomposition rate increases.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Compression favours fewer gas molecules. Kc is unchanged. Equilibrium shifts right, while increased [SO₃] also raises its decomposition rate. Thus (2) and (3).","tip":"Compression changes position and rates, not Kc.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/26","sourceIndex":21,"stem":"CO+H₂O ⇌ CO₂+H₂, ΔH=−41.1 kJ mol⁻¹, is at equilibrium at fixed volume. Which is correct when temperature increases?","context":"","options":["Pressure remains unchanged","Both forward and backward rates increase","Kc remains unchanged","Yields of CO₂ and H₂ increase equally"],"answer":1,"why":"Heating increases both reaction rates. Both rate constants increase. Pressure and Kc change, and the exothermic equilibrium shifts left rather than increasing products.","tip":"Heating affects kinetics and equilibrium.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/36","sourceIndex":22,"stem":"1st statement: At fixed temperature, increasing reactant concentration changes Kc.\n2nd statement: It increases forward rate but decreases backward rate.","context":"","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement correctly explains the 1st.","Both statements are true but the 2nd statement does not correctly explain the 1st.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":3,"why":"Concentration affects Q and rates, not Kc. Kc stays constant. Immediately after adding reactant, the forward rate rises while the backward rate is initially unchanged, not decreased. Both statements are false.","tip":"Separate immediate rates from later shifting.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/33","sourceIndex":23,"stem":"H₂O ⇌ H⁺+OH⁻ is endothermic. Which are correct when pure water is heated from 25 to 50 °C?\n(1) [OH⁻] remains 1.0×10⁻⁷.\n(2) pH is below 7.\n(3) Water remains neutral.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Neutral means [H⁺]=[OH⁻], not always pH 7. Heating shifts ionisation right. pH falls below 7, but equal H⁺ and OH⁻ keep the water neutral. (2) and (3) are correct.","tip":"Neutral pH need not equal 7 away from 25 °C.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/26","sourceIndex":24,"stem":"For SO₂+NO₂ ⇌ SO₃+NO, ΔH=−42 kJ mol⁻¹, which change increases equilibrium NO?","context":"","options":["Decrease temperature","Decrease volume","Add SO₃","Remove SO₂"],"answer":0,"why":"Cooling favours the exothermic forward reaction. Lower temperature shifts right. Equal gas moles make volume ineffective; adding product or removing reactant shifts left.","tip":"Treat heat as a product for exothermic reactions.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/26","sourceIndex":25,"stem":"Acetone+HCN ⇌ cyanohydrin has ΔH>0 at fixed volume. Which statement is correct?","context":"","options":["Adding product increases Kc","Increasing temperature increases product concentration","Reactant and product concentrations must be equal","After adding HCN its concentration returns exactly to the old value"],"answer":1,"why":"Heating favours the endothermic forward reaction. Higher temperature shifts right. Composition does not change Kc; concentrations need not be equal; an addition is not completely cancelled.","tip":"Use ΔH before considering composition.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/35","sourceIndex":26,"stem":"1st statement: Increasing temperature can increase yield for all reversible reactions.\n2nd statement: Increasing temperature can shorten time to equilibrium for all reversible reactions.","context":"","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement correctly explains the 1st.","Both statements are true but the 2nd statement does not correctly explain the 1st.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":2,"why":"Temperature raises rates but shifts yield according to ΔH. The first statement is false for exothermic forward reactions. The second is true because both directions speed up.","tip":"Separate kinetics from thermodynamics.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/27","sourceIndex":27,"stem":"Br₂(aq)+H₂O ⇌ HOBr+H⁺+Br⁻. Which addition makes the bromine colour paler?","context":"","options":["HCl(g)","HBr(g)","NaBr(s)","NaOH(s)"],"answer":3,"why":"Remove H⁺ to shift toward colourless HOBr. NaOH removes H⁺, shifting right and consuming coloured Br₂.","tip":"Identify the coloured species first.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/32","sourceIndex":28,"stem":"For W+X ⇌ Y+Z, ΔH=+45 kJ mol⁻¹, which increase equilibrium Y?\n(1) Remove Z.\n(2) Increase volume.\n(3) Increase temperature.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":2,"why":"Remove product or heat an endothermic reaction. Removing Z and heating shift right. Equal total coefficients make volume ineffective. Thus (1) and (3).","tip":"Apply each change separately.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/32","sourceIndex":29,"stem":"HA ⇌ H⁺+A⁻, ΔH>0; HA is colourless and A⁻ pink. Which statements are correct?\n(1) Na₂CO₃ makes it paler.\n(2) Heating makes it darker.\n(3) HCl increases [A⁻].","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"Darker means higher [A⁻]. Carbonate removes H⁺ and actually shifts right; heating also shifts right; HCl shifts left. Only statement (2) is correct as worded.","tip":"Translate colour into concentration.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/34","sourceIndex":30,"stem":"For PCl₅ ⇌ PCl₃+Cl₂, which increase equilibrium PCl₃?\n(1) Add catalyst.\n(2) Remove Cl₂.\n(3) Increase volume.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":2,"why":"Remove product or lower pressure to favour more gas molecules. Removing Cl₂ and increasing volume shift right. A catalyst does not change composition.","tip":"Count gas coefficients.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/25/1A/27","sourceIndex":31,"stem":"For 2Y ⇌ Y₂, Kc=6.2 and Qc=4.5 at time t. Which is correct?","context":"","options":["System is at equilibrium","Forward rate is greater than backward rate","[Y₂] is 4.5 times [Y]","[Y] starts to increase"],"answer":1,"why":"Compare Qc with Kc. Qc<Kc, so the system forms more Y₂ and the forward rate exceeds the backward rate.","tip":"Qc<Kc means forward.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/31","sourceIndex":32,"stem":"For 2X₃ ⇌ 3X₂, Kc=4.0 and Qc=2.0. Which are correct?\n(1) Forward rate exceeds backward.\n(2) Qc will decrease.\n(3) [X₂] must equal [X₃].","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Qc below Kc drives the reaction forward. Forward rate is greater and Qc rises toward Kc. Equal concentrations are not required. Only (1).","tip":"State whether Q must rise or fall.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/32","sourceIndex":33,"stem":"For X ⇌ Y, equilibrium X:Y=1:3. Which statements are correct?\n(1) Starting with X only, Q>K before equilibrium.\n(2) Starting with Y only gives the same equilibrium ratio.\n(3) At equilibrium backward rate is three times forward.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"At fixed temperature K fixes the ratio; equilibrium rates are equal. K=[Y]/[X]=3. Starting with X gives Q=0<K. Starting with Y reaches the same ratio, and rates are equal. Only (2).","tip":"Equilibrium composition is path independent.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/29","sourceIndex":34,"stem":"For 2SO₂+O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, ΔH<0, what happens to SO₃ when (i) temperature rises at constant pressure and (ii) pressure rises at constant temperature?","context":"","options":["increase; decrease","increase; increase","decrease; increase","decrease; decrease"],"answer":2,"why":"Heat favours reverse; pressure favours fewer gas molecules. Higher temperature shifts left and decreases SO₃. Higher pressure shifts right and increases SO₃.","tip":"Treat the two disturbances independently.","strand":""},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/31","sourceIndex":35,"stem":"Br₂+H₂O ⇌ HOBr+H⁺+Br⁻; HOBr is colourless. Which make the mixture paler?\n(1) Add KOH.\n(2) Pass HCl.\n(3) Add NaBr.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Paler means less Br₂, so shift right. KOH removes H⁺ and shifts right. HCl and NaBr add product ions and shift left. Only (1).","tip":"Identify which side is coloured.","strand":""}]},{"topic":11,"name":"Chemistry of Carbon Compounds","href":"chemistry-of-carbon-compounds.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/19/1A/23","sourceIndex":7,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning ethanol are correct? \n(1) It is flammable. \n(2) It is soluble in water. \n(3) It is more volatile than water.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"","strand":"Fundamentals & functional groups"},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/9","sourceIndex":9,"stem":"Consider the following three compounds: \nX: CH ₃ CH ₂ CH ₂ CH ₂ OH Y: HOCH ₂ CH ₂ OH Z: CH ₃ COOCH ₃ \nWhich of the following shows the decreasing order of their solubilities in water?","context":"","options":["X > Y > Z","Z > Y > X","Y > Z > X","Y > X > Z"],"answer":2,"why":"","strand":"Fundamentals & functional groups"},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/26","sourceIndex":13,"stem":"How many geometrical isomers does H ₃ C-CH=CH-CH=CH-CH ₃ have?","context":"","options":["0","2","3","4"],"answer":2,"why":"","strand":"Isomerism"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/29","sourceIndex":21,"stem":"The molecular formula of compound X is C ₄ H ₄ O ₄ . It has two –COOH groups. How many isomers may X have?","context":"","options":["5","4","3","2"],"answer":2,"why":"","strand":"Isomerism"},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/29","sourceIndex":28,"stem":"An acyclic hydrocarbon R contains C=C bond as the only functional group. 0.4 mol of R completely reacts with 19.2 dm ³ of hydrogen gas at room conditions. How many C=C bond(s) may R have? \n(Molar volume of gas at room conditions = 24 dm ³ )","context":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":1,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/6","sourceIndex":30,"stem":"What is the product of the reaction between chloroethene and bromine dissolved in an organic solvent?","context":"","options":["2-chloro-1,2-dibromoethane","1,2-dibromo-1-chloroethane","2-chloro-1,1-dibromoethane","2,2-dibromo-1-chloroethane"],"answer":1,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/29","sourceIndex":31,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning compound U (CH ₃ CH ₂ CH=CHCH ₂ CH ₂ OH) is correct?","context":"","options":["The empirical formula of U is C ₃ H ₆ O.","The systematic name of U is hex-4-en-1-ol.","U reacts with HCl to give a single product.","U can separately turn Br ₂ (aq) and acidified KMnO ₄ (aq) colourless."],"answer":3,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/33","sourceIndex":33,"stem":"Which of the following compounds can be formed when (CH ₃ ) ₂ C(OH)CH ₂ CH ₃ is dehydrated? \n(1) (CH ₃ ) ₂ C=CHCH ₃ \n(2) (CH ₃ ) ₂ CHCH=CH ₂ \n(3) CH ₂ =C(CH ₃ )CH ₂ CH ₃","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":1,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/28","sourceIndex":40,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning but-1-ene and butan-1-ol is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["Both of them can decolourise acidified KMnO ₄ (aq).","Butan-1-ol can react with PBr ₃ (l) while but-1-ene cannot.","Both of them can react with H ₂ (g) in the presence of platinum.","But-1-ene can be obtained from heating butan-1-ol with Al ₂ O ₃ (s)."],"answer":2,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/29","sourceIndex":45,"stem":"Consider the following reaction: \nHOOCCH ₂ COCH ₂ CHO NaBH ₄ (aq) → Y \nWhat is Y?","context":"","options":["HOOCCH ₂ COCH ₂ CH ₂ OH","HOOCCH ₂ CH(OH)CH ₂ CHO","HOOCCH ₂ CH(OH)CH ₂ CH ₂ OH","HOCH ₂ CH ₂ CH(OH)CH ₂ CH ₂ OH"],"answer":2,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/35","sourceIndex":48,"stem":"Which of the following processes can form ethanol? \n(1) heating ethanoic acid with NaBH ₄ \n(2) heating bromoethane with KOH(aq) \n(3) heating ethyl butanoate with NaOH(aq) under reflux","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":2,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/32","sourceIndex":49,"stem":"Which of the following statements about the action of sodium hydroxide solution on ethanamide is/are correct? \n(1) Sodium ethanoate is formed in the reaction. \n(2) In the reaction, sodium hydroxide acts as a catalyst. \n(3) The reaction attains equilibrium if the reaction mixture is heated under reflux.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/30","sourceIndex":52,"stem":"Which of the following pairs of substances would NOT react with each other?","context":"","options":["ethene and hydrogen chloride","propan-1-ol and phosphorus trichloride","propene and hydrogen in the presence of platinum","methylpropan-2-ol and acidified potassium dichromate solution"],"answer":3,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/34","sourceIndex":53,"stem":"Which of the following mixtures would NOT separate into two liquid layers after heating under reflux for a period of time? \n(1) HCOOCH ₂ CH ₃ (l) and excess NaOH(aq) \n(2) CH ₃ CH ₂ CH ₂ Cl(l) and excess concentrated NaOH(aq) \n(3) CH ₃ CH ₂ CHO(l) and excess acidified K ₂ Cr ₂ O ₇ (aq)","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":3,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/19","sourceIndex":54,"stem":"Which of the following pairs of substances can be distinguished by using acidified KMnO ₄ (aq)? \n(1) pent-1-ene and pent-2-ene \n(2) cyclohexane and cyclohexene \n(3) polyethene and poly(chloroethene)","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/26","sourceIndex":66,"stem":"Propanoic acid can react with propan-2-ol to give an ester X. In an experiment, 30.0 g of propanoic acid reacts with excess propan-2-ol to give 6.32 g of X. What is the percentage yield of X? \n(Relative atomic masses: H = 1.0, C = 12.0, O = 16.0)","context":"","options":["21.1%","15.3%","13.4%","12.0%"],"answer":2,"why":"","strand":"Reactions & synthesis"},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/32","sourceIndex":69,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning aspirin is / are correct? \n(1) It undergoes esterification with ethanoic acid in the presence of an acid catalyst. \n(2) It reacts with sodium carbonate solution to give a colourless gas. \n(3) It can be used to reduce inflammation.","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"","strand":"Important organic substances"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/35","sourceIndex":70,"stem":"Soap can \n(1) be made from fats. \n(2) emulsify oily particles. \n(3) increase the surface tension of water. \nWhich of the following combinations is correct?","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":0,"why":"","strand":"Important organic substances"},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/34","sourceIndex":71,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning soap are correct? \n(1) Soap is an ester. \n(2) Soap can reduce the surface tension of water. \n(3) Soap particles consist of both hydrophobic and hydrophilic parts.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":2,"why":"","strand":"Important organic substances"},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/31","sourceIndex":79,"stem":"Which of the following compounds CANNOT form condensation polymers? \n(1) H ₂ N(CH ₂ ) ₅ CO ₂ H \n(2) CH ₃ CO ₂ CH=CH ₂ \n(3) CH ₃ CH(OH)CO ₂ H","context":"","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":1,"why":"","strand":"Polymers"},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/34","sourceIndex":80,"stem":"Which of the following compounds can be used as a monomer for condensation polymerization? \n(1) H ₂ C=CHCH ₂ CH ₂ CH=CH ₂ \n(2) HOOCCH ₂ CH ₂ CH ₂ CH ₂ COOH \n(3) HOCH ₂ CH ₂ CH ₂ CH ₂ CH ₂ CH ₂ OH","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":2,"why":"","strand":"Polymers"},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/34","sourceIndex":85,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning nylon-6,6 are correct? \n(1) Fishing net can be made from nylon-6,6. \n(2) H ₂ N(CH ₂ ) ₆ NH ₂ is one of the monomers of nylon-6,6. \n(3) The intermolecular attractions in nylon-6,6 are covalent bonds.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":0,"why":"","strand":"Polymers"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/35","sourceIndex":87,"stem":"P and Q are polymers made from the same monomers. The average number of repeating units in P is 5000 and that in Q is 80000. Which of the following statements are correct? \n(1) Q melts at a higher temperature than P. \n(2) The average molecular mass of Q is greater than that of P. \n(3) The number of carbon atoms in a repeating unit of Q is greater than that of P.","context":"","options":["(1) and (2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only","(1), (2) and (3)"],"answer":0,"why":"","strand":"Polymers"}]},{"topic":12,"name":"Patterns in the Chemical World","href":"patterns-in-the-chemical-world.html","questions":[{"source":"DSE/18/1A/28","sourceIndex":0,"stem":"Which of the following statements is correct?","context":"","options":["The boiling point of argon is lower than that of neon.","The boiling point of nitrogen is lower than that of oxygen.","The melting point of silicon is lower than that of sodium.","The melting point of aluminium is lower than that of magnesium."],"answer":1,"why":"Nitrogen has a lower boiling point than oxygen. An N₂ molecule has fewer electrons and a smaller electron cloud than an O₂ molecule, so the London dispersion forces between N₂ molecules are weaker. Less energy is needed to separate them.","tip":"Relate boiling points of simple substances to electron-cloud size, and melting points to structure and bonding.","strand":"Periodic trends in physical properties"},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/35","sourceIndex":1,"stem":"Consider the following statements and choose the best answer:","context":"1st statement The melting point of silicon is higher than that of aluminium.\n2nd statement The number of electrons in a silicon atom is greater than that in an aluminium atom.","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":1,"why":"Both statements are true, but the second does not explain the first. Silicon has a giant covalent structure whereas aluminium has a giant metallic structure. Silicon's higher melting point is explained by the energy needed to break many strong covalent bonds, not merely by its atom having one more electron.","tip":"For assertion–reason questions, test truth and causation separately.","strand":"Structure, bonding and melting point"},{"source":"DSE/15/1A/25","sourceIndex":5,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning the Periodic Table is correct?","context":"","options":["The melting point of the Group I elements increases down the group.","The boiling point of the Group VII elements increases down the group.","The elements are arranged in the order of increasing relative atomic mass.","The electrical conductivity of the third period elements increases from left to right."],"answer":1,"why":"The boiling points of Group VII elements increase down the group. Halogen molecules become larger and more polarisable down the group, so London dispersion forces become stronger and boiling points rise.","tip":"Separate group trends from period trends before evaluating each statement.","strand":"Group and period trends"},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/31","sourceIndex":6,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning the elements in the Periodic Table is correct?","context":"","options":["The electrical conductivities of metals in the third period decrease from left to right.","The melting points of the Group I elements increase down the group.","The atomic sizes of noble gases decrease down the group.","The boiling points of halogens increase down the group."],"answer":3,"why":"Halogen boiling points increase down the group. The electron clouds of halogen molecules become larger and more polarisable down Group VII, strengthening London dispersion forces.","tip":"Down a group, added electron shells increase particle size and usually strengthen dispersion forces.","strand":"Group and period trends"},{"source":"DSE/23/1A/33","sourceIndex":7,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning the elements across the second period of the Periodic Table going from left to right is / are correct?","context":"(1) The melting point of carbon is the highest among these elements.\n(2) The electrical conductivity of lithium is the highest among these elements.\n(3) The bond type of these elements changes from ionic bonding to covalent bonding.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Only statement (1) is correct. Carbon has a giant covalent structure and the highest melting point. Lithium is not the best electrical conductor in Period 2, and the bonding changes from metallic to covalent—not from ionic to covalent.","tip":"Do not confuse the bonding within elemental solids with bonding in their compounds.","strand":"Period 2 physical properties"},{"source":"DSE/12/1A/31","sourceIndex":8,"stem":"Which of the following oxides would form an acidic solution when added to water?","context":"","options":["carbon dioxide","silicon dioxide","aluminium oxide","lithium oxide"],"answer":0,"why":"Carbon dioxide forms an acidic solution in water. Carbon dioxide dissolves and reacts with water to form carbonic acid. Silicon dioxide and aluminium oxide are insoluble in water, while lithium oxide reacts with water to form alkaline lithium hydroxide.","tip":"An acidic oxide does not necessarily form an acidic solution if it is insoluble in water.","strand":"Acid-base properties of oxides"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/30","sourceIndex":9,"stem":"Which of the following trends involving Na, Mg and Al is INCORRECT?","context":"","options":["Melting point of metal: Al > Mg > Na","Electronegativity of metal: Al > Mg > Na","Metal reactivity with water: Na > Mg > Al","Base strength of metal oxide: Al₂O₃ > MgO > Na₂O"],"answer":3,"why":"The stated order of oxide basicity is reversed. Across Period 3, metallic character and oxide basicity decrease. The correct basic-strength order is Na₂O > MgO > Al₂O₃, with Al₂O₃ amphoteric.","tip":"Across Period 3, oxides change from basic through amphoteric to acidic.","strand":"Period 3 trends"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/28","sourceIndex":10,"stem":"Which of the following statements correctly describes the property of an amphoteric oxide?","context":"","options":["It can react as an acid or as a base.","It can react with water to form an acid and an alkali.","It can be simultaneously oxidized and reduced in a reaction.","It can react with water to form an oxidizing agent and a reducing agent."],"answer":0,"why":"An amphoteric oxide can react as an acid or as a base. An amphoteric oxide reacts with both acids and alkalis. The term describes acid-base behaviour, not simultaneous redox behaviour or reaction with water.","tip":"Amphoteric means reacting with both acids and bases.","strand":"Amphoteric oxides"},{"source":"DSE/14/1A/36","sourceIndex":11,"stem":"Consider the following statements and choose the best answer:","context":"1st statement Aluminium oxide is soluble in water.\n2nd statement Aluminium oxide is an amphoteric oxide.","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":2,"why":"Aluminium oxide is insoluble in water but amphoteric. Al₂O₃ does not dissolve in water. It is amphoteric because it reacts with both acids and strong alkalis.","tip":"Solubility in water and amphoteric behaviour are separate properties.","strand":"Aluminium oxide"},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/36","sourceIndex":12,"stem":"Consider the following statements and choose the best answer:","context":"1st statement Both aluminium oxide and magnesium oxide exhibit similar acid-base properties.\n2nd statement Both aluminium oxide and magnesium oxide are ionic oxides.","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":2,"why":"Their acid-base properties differ, although both are ionic oxides. Aluminium oxide is amphoteric, whereas magnesium oxide is basic. Both are classified as giant ionic oxides in the syllabus treatment.","tip":"Similar structure does not guarantee identical acid-base behaviour.","strand":"Acid-base properties of oxides"},{"source":"DSE/17/1A/30","sourceIndex":13,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning silicon dioxide solid is correct?","context":"","options":["There are single covalent bonds between silicon atoms and oxygen atoms.","It is insoluble in sodium hydroxide solution.","It has a simple molecular structure.","It conducts electricity at room temperature."],"answer":0,"why":"Silicon dioxide contains single covalent Si–O bonds. SiO₂ has a giant covalent network of strong single Si–O bonds. It can react with strong alkalis, is not molecular and has no mobile charged particles at room temperature.","tip":"Use structure to predict both melting point and electrical conductivity.","strand":"Silicon dioxide"},{"source":"DSE/16/1A/36","sourceIndex":14,"stem":"Consider the following statements and choose the best answer:","context":"1st statement P₄O₁₀(s) can react with NaOH(aq).\n2nd statement P₄O₁₀(s) is an acidic oxide.","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":0,"why":"Both statements are true, and the second explains the first. P₄O₁₀ is an acidic oxide, so it reacts with the base NaOH to form phosphate salt and water.","tip":"Acidic oxides react with bases to form salt and water.","strand":"Acidic oxides"},{"source":"DSE/20/1A/28","sourceIndex":15,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning the oxides of elements in the third period of the Periodic Table is correct?","context":"","options":["SiO₂(s) dissolves in water to form a neutral solution.","P₄O₁₀(s) dissolves in water to form an acidic solution.","Al₂O₃(s) dissolves in water to form an alkaline solution.","Cl₂O(g) dissolves in water to form Cl₂(aq) and O₂(g) only."],"answer":1,"why":"Phosphorus(V) oxide forms an acidic solution in water. P₄O₁₀ reacts with water to form phosphoric acid. SiO₂ and Al₂O₃ are insoluble in water, and Cl₂O reacts with water to form HOCl.","tip":"Distinguish an oxide's acid-base character from whether it actually dissolves in water.","strand":"Period 3 oxides"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/33","sourceIndex":16,"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning the elements in the third period of the Periodic Table going from Na to Cl is / are correct?","context":"(1) The bond type of the elements changes from metallic bonding to covalent bonding.\n(2) The oxide of the elements changes from acidic to basic.\n(3) The electrical conductivity of the elements keeps decreasing.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Only statement (1) is correct. The elements change from metallic structures to covalent structures. Their oxides change from basic to amphoteric and then acidic, while conductivity first increases from Na to Al and then drops.","tip":"Treat bonding, oxide character and conductivity as three separate Period 3 trends.","strand":"Period 3 elements and oxides"},{"source":"DSE/13/1A/26","sourceIndex":19,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic property of transition metals?","context":"","options":["They form coloured compounds.","They exhibit variable oxidation numbers in their compounds.","They react with dilute hydrochloric acid to give hydrogen gas.","They exhibit catalytic property in elemental state or as compounds."],"answer":2,"why":"Reaction with dilute hydrochloric acid is not a general transition-metal characteristic. Some transition metals, including copper, silver and gold, do not displace hydrogen from dilute hydrochloric acid. Coloured compounds, variable oxidation states and catalytic activity are characteristic properties.","tip":"A characteristic property must be broadly associated with transition metals, not merely true for selected reactive metals.","strand":"Transition-metal characteristics"},{"source":"DSE/19/1A/33","sourceIndex":20,"stem":"Which of the following does NOT exhibit a characteristic of iron as a transition metal?","context":"","options":["Iron corrodes readily.","Iron can be used as a catalyst.","Iron can form two chlorides.","Iron(II) sulphate solution is green."],"answer":0,"why":"Corrosion does not demonstrate a transition-metal characteristic. Catalytic activity, variable oxidation states and coloured compounds are characteristic of transition metals. Corrosion is a chemical behaviour of iron but not a defining transition-metal characteristic.","tip":"Link each observation to the standard list: coloured compounds, variable oxidation states and catalysis.","strand":"Iron as a transition metal"},{"source":"DSE/21/1A/27","sourceIndex":21,"stem":"Copper(II) oxide can catalyse the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide to form oxygen and water. In an experiment, hydrogen peroxide solution is shaken with copper(II) oxide in a test tube. What would be observed after completion of the reaction?","context":"","options":["a pale blue liquid","a blue solid and a colourless liquid","a black solid and a colourless liquid","a reddish brown solid and a colourless liquid"],"answer":2,"why":"A black solid and a colourless liquid remain. A catalyst is regenerated and remains chemically unchanged at the end. Copper(II) oxide is black, while the remaining aqueous liquid is colourless after hydrogen peroxide decomposes to water and oxygen.","tip":"A catalyst may participate in intermediate steps but is regenerated at the end.","strand":"Transition-metal catalysis"},{"source":"DSE/22/1A/32","sourceIndex":22,"stem":"Consider the following equilibrium system:","context":"2CrO₄²−(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) ⇌ Cr₂O₇²−(aq) + H₂O(l)\n(1) Cr₂O₇²−(aq) ions are orange in colour.\n(2) Adding HCl(aq) would shift the equilibrium position to the right.\n(3) The oxidation states of chromium in CrO₄²− and Cr₂O₇²− are the same.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Only statement (1) demonstrates a transition-metal characteristic. The orange colour of dichromate ions demonstrates coloured transition-metal compounds. The acid-driven equilibrium shift is an application of Le Chatelier's principle, and chromium remains at +6 in both ions, so neither demonstrates variable oxidation states.","tip":"The question asks what demonstrates a characteristic, not merely which statements are true.","strand":"Transition-metal characteristics"},{"source":"DSE/18/1A/32","sourceIndex":23,"stem":"Which of the following processes can illustrate the characteristics of transition metals?","context":"(1) mixing AgNO₃(aq) and NaCl(aq)\n(2) mixing FeSO₄(aq) and Br₂(aq)\n(3) mixing CuSO₄(s) and H₂O(l)","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":3,"why":"Processes (2) and (3) illustrate transition-metal characteristics. Bromine oxidises Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺, showing variable oxidation states. Dissolving copper(II) sulfate gives a blue solution, showing coloured compounds. Silver chloride precipitation is not a characteristic transition-metal property.","tip":"Identify exactly which standard characteristic each process displays.","strand":"Transition-metal characteristics"},{"source":"DSE/24/1A/36","sourceIndex":24,"stem":"Consider the following statements and choose the best answer:","context":"1st statement Iron exhibits variable oxidation states in its compounds.\n2nd statement Iron forms coloured compounds.","options":["Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","Both statements are true and the 2nd statement is NOT a correct explanation of the 1st statement.","The 1st statement is false but the 2nd statement is true.","Both statements are false."],"answer":1,"why":"Both statements are true, but the second does not explain the first. Iron commonly forms Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ compounds and many iron compounds are coloured. These are separate consequences of partially filled d orbitals; coloured compounds do not cause variable oxidation states.","tip":"Two correct transition-metal properties do not automatically form a cause-and-effect pair.","strand":"Iron compounds"},{"source":"DSE/26/1A/32","sourceIndex":26,"stem":"Which of the following statements about SiO₂(s) and P₄O₁₀(s) is / are correct?","context":"(1) Both of them are acidic oxides.\n(2) The melting point of P₄O₁₀(s) is higher than that of SiO₂(s).\n(3) Both of them can dissolve in water to form an acidic solution.","options":["(1) only","(2) only","(1) and (3) only","(2) and (3) only"],"answer":0,"why":"Only statement (1) is correct. Both are acidic oxides. SiO₂ has a giant covalent structure and a much higher melting point than molecular P₄O₁₀. P₄O₁₀ reacts with water to form an acidic solution, but SiO₂ is insoluble.","tip":"Compare structure and solubility separately from acid-base classification.","strand":"Period 3 oxides"}]}];
